INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('ed37eb49-1675-42b3-b22d-d36806c52f77', 'review', 'en', 'bitbox02', 'review-bitbox02', 'BitBox02 Review: Open-Source Simplicity with a Bitcoin-Only Option', 'BitBox02 Review (2026): Security, Backup & Who Should Buy It', 'Our BitBox02 review covers its open-source dual-chip security, microSD backups, Bitcoin-only edition, limitations and the best alternatives.', 'A compact, open-source wallet that makes good security habits easy — as long as you don''t need iPhone support.', '## Overview
The BitBox02 from Swiss company Shift Crypto is a small USB-C device with touch-sensitive edges instead of buttons. It comes in two editions: **Multi**, which supports Bitcoin and a curated set of other assets, and **Bitcoin-only**, which runs firmware stripped down to Bitcoin. Both editions use the same hardware.
What sets it apart is not one headline feature but a combination of sensible defaults: open-source firmware and hardware, a secure chip, a microSD-card backup that removes the most error-prone part of set-up for many beginners, and companion software (the BitBoxApp) that keeps things simple.
## Security architecture
The BitBox02 uses a **dual-chip design**. A general-purpose microcontroller runs the open-source firmware; a separate secure chip stores secrets that are needed to decrypt the wallet and enforces limits on PIN attempts. The idea is to get the auditability of open-source firmware *and* some of the physical-attack resistance of a secure element, without depending on a secure element''s closed operating system for everything.
Two further points matter:
- **Bitcoin-only firmware.** Less code means fewer places for bugs to hide. If you only hold BTC, this is a meaningful reduction in attack surface.
- **No radios.** The original BitBox02 has no Bluetooth or NFC. Everything happens over a USB-C cable, which some users prefer for simplicity.
The trade-off is that it is **not air-gapped**: the device signs while connected to a computer or Android phone. For most users this is fine — the device displays what you are signing and asks for confirmation — but if you specifically want microSD- or QR-based signing, look at the alternatives below.
## Setup and user experience
Set-up runs through the BitBoxApp on desktop or Android. You choose a device password on the BitBox02 itself, then create a backup. The touch-slider interface takes a few minutes to get used to; after that, confirming addresses and transactions is quick. The screen is small, which makes reviewing long addresses slower than on touchscreen devices.
## Supported cryptocurrencies
The Bitcoin-only edition supports Bitcoin (including testnet). The Multi edition adds a deliberately limited list of other assets such as Ethereum and ERC-20 tokens and Litecoin; check Shift Crypto''s current list before buying if you need a specific coin. If you want hundreds of chains, a Ledger or Trezor is a better fit.
## Backup and recovery
This is the BitBox02''s strongest area. You can back up to a **microSD card** (stored somewhere safe, separate from the device), write down the standard **24-word BIP39 recovery phrase**, or both. Because the phrase is a standard, you can recover in other BIP39-compatible wallets if Shift Crypto ever disappeared. **Optional passphrase** support lets you create hidden wallets.
A microSD card is convenient but is a digital medium: store it carefully, keep more than one copy if you rely on it, and consider a paper or metal copy of the words for long-term storage. See our guide to [backing up Bitcoin securely](/en/learn/how-to-back-up-bitcoin).
## Privacy considerations
The BitBoxApp can connect to your own Bitcoin node and supports Tor, which reduces what third-party servers learn about your addresses. The device also works with independent software such as Sparrow and Electrum.
## Pricing and value
At its recommended price (check the live price — ours is shown with its verification status above), the BitBox02 sits in the mid-range. You are paying for a polished experience and a transparent security model rather than for a large screen or wireless features. For open-source buyers, that is good value.
## Compared with alternatives
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,ledger-nano-s-plus,bitbox02-nova]]
- **Trezor Safe 3** offers a similar open-source approach with a secure element at a lower price, plus multi-share backups — but no microSD option.
- **Ledger Nano S Plus** supports far more assets, but its secure-element operating system is closed source.
- **BitBox02 Nova** is the same idea with Bluetooth for iPhone users and a nicer screen, at a higher price.
Read our head-to-head: [BitBox02 vs Ledger Nano X](/en/compare/bitbox02-vs-ledger-nano-x) and [COLDCARD Q vs BitBox02](/en/compare/coldcard-q-vs-bitbox02).
## Final verdict
The BitBox02 makes it easy to do the right things: verify on the device, keep a good backup, and run open-source software. It is an excellent first hardware wallet for desktop and Android users — especially in its Bitcoin-only edition — and a solid choice for experienced users who want a simple single-signature or multisig signer. iPhone users should consider the Nova; those who want air-gapped signing should look at COLDCARD, Jade or Keystone.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Yes. Shift Crypto publishes the firmware and the hardware design files, so independent researchers can review them.", "q": "Is the BitBox02 open source?"}, {"a": "The original BitBox02 connects over USB and is not supported on iPhone. The BitBox02 Nova adds Bluetooth for iPhone and iPad.", "q": "Does the BitBox02 work with an iPhone?"}, {"a": "Yes. You can record the standard 24-word BIP39 recovery phrase instead of, or as well as, the microSD backup.", "q": "Can I use the BitBox02 without the microSD card?"}, {"a": "If you only hold Bitcoin, the Bitcoin-only edition runs less code and therefore has a smaller attack surface. Choose Multi only if you need the other supported assets.", "q": "Bitcoin-only or Multi edition?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://bitbox.swiss/bitbox02/", "label": "Shift Crypto — BitBox02"}], "verdict": "The BitBox02 is one of the easiest ways to get open-source, secure-element-backed self-custody right. Its microSD backup and Bitcoin-only edition are genuine strengths. Choose the Nova instead if you use an iPhone, and look elsewhere if you need air-gapped signing."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.966755+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.849405+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('b79444a0-d8a1-4767-9175-0900ae97a5fb', 'review', 'en', 'trezor-safe-5', 'review-trezor-safe-5', 'Trezor Safe 5 Review: Open Source Meets a Proper Touchscreen', 'Trezor Safe 5 Review (2026): Security, Touchscreen & Alternatives', 'Trezor Safe 5 review: open-source firmware with a secure element, colour touchscreen, multi-share backups, limitations and who should buy it.', 'Trezor''s open-source security model with a far more comfortable screen — the main gap is iPhone support.', '## Overview
Trezor, made by Prague-based SatoshiLabs, introduced the first consumer hardware wallet and has always published its firmware as open source. The **Safe 5** combines that approach with a secure element and a colour touchscreen with haptic feedback. It sits between the budget Safe 3 and the Bluetooth-equipped flagship Safe 7.
## Security architecture
Older Trezor models (the Model One and Model T) stored secrets on a general-purpose chip, and researchers demonstrated that with physical access and specialist equipment the seed could be extracted — which is why Trezor long recommended a passphrase for high-value storage. The **Safe series adds a secure element**: a secret held in the secure element, combined with your PIN, is required to decrypt the seed. This makes physical extraction far harder while keeping the main firmware open source.
The secure element''s own internal firmware is not open source — no mainstream secure-element vendor allows that — but it does not run the wallet logic. Trezor also offers **Bitcoin-only firmware** for users who want the smallest attack surface.
The Safe 5 is **USB-only**: no Bluetooth, no NFC, and no air-gapped signing.
## Setup and user experience
The touchscreen is the reason to pick the Safe 5 over the Safe 3. Entering a PIN or passphrase on the device itself — rather than on a computer — is much easier on a touchscreen, and reading full addresses before confirming a transaction is more comfortable. Setup is guided by Trezor Suite on desktop, web or Android.
## Supported cryptocurrencies
The Universal firmware supports Bitcoin and a large range of other coins and tokens through Trezor Suite and third-party wallets such as MetaMask. The Bitcoin-only firmware supports BTC only.
## Backup and recovery
Trezor''s Safe series supports **SLIP-39 backups**, including **multi-share** backups that split your recovery across several shares with a threshold (e.g. any 2 of 3). That is a powerful option for people who want geographic redundancy without a single point of failure. Standard BIP39 recovery phrases are also supported, and **passphrases** let you create hidden wallets.
## Privacy considerations
Trezor Suite supports connecting to your own node and routing traffic via Tor. As an open-source vendor, Trezor''s code can be reviewed independently. The device works with Sparrow, Electrum and other privacy-focused wallets.
## Pricing and value
The Safe 5 costs noticeably more than the Safe 3, which has the same security model. You are paying for the touchscreen and haptics. Whether that is worth it depends on how often you will use the device: for frequent use and on-device passphrase entry, it is.
## Compared with alternatives
[[table:trezor-safe-5,trezor-safe-3,ledger-flex,bitbox02]]
- **Trezor Safe 3** — same security model, buttons instead of a touchscreen, lower price.
- **Ledger Flex** — bigger E Ink screen, Bluetooth and broader asset support, but closed secure-element OS.
- **BitBox02** — open-source alternative with microSD backup and a smaller form factor.
See also [Trezor Safe 5 vs Ledger Flex](/en/compare/trezor-safe-5-vs-ledger-flex).
## Final verdict
The Trezor Safe 5 is a well-balanced device for people who want open-source firmware without sacrificing comfort. Its multi-share backup option is a standout for long-term storage. If you mainly use an iPhone, look at the Safe 7; if you want to save money, the Safe 3 gives you the same core protections.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{trezor-safe-5}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Yes. Like the Safe 3, it pairs open-source firmware with a secure element that protects a secret needed to unlock the wallet.", "q": "Does the Trezor Safe 5 have a secure element?"}, {"a": "A SLIP-39 backup split into several shares, of which a set number (for example 2 of 3) is needed to recover. It lets you store backups in different places without any single share being enough to steal your funds.", "q": "What is a multi-share backup?"}, {"a": "Not for signing over a cable connection. Trezor''s Bluetooth model, the Safe 7, supports iOS.", "q": "Can I use the Safe 5 with an iPhone?"}, {"a": "Yes. Trezor offers Bitcoin-only firmware, which reduces the code running on the device.", "q": "Is there a Bitcoin-only version?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://trezor.io/", "label": "Trezor — official site"}], "verdict": "The Safe 5 is the most comfortable way into Trezor''s open-source ecosystem: a secure element, multi-share backups and a touchscreen that makes on-device verification easy. iPhone users should look at the Safe 7; budget buyers get the same security model in the Safe 3."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.968732+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.851313+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('a64cba54-c010-49da-bffa-3dd4d333bc74', 'review', 'en', 'coldcard-q', 'review-coldcard-q', 'COLDCARD Q Review: Powerful Air-Gapped Signer After a Serious 2026 Flaw', 'COLDCARD Q Review (2026): Features, Seed Flaw & What To Do', 'COLDCARD Q review: air-gapped Bitcoin-only design, QR and microSD signing, the 2026 seed-generation flaw, migration steps and alternatives.', 'Feature-for-feature one of the most capable Bitcoin signers — but the 2026 entropy flaw changes how we rate it.', '> **Security advisory (2026):** Coinkite disclosed a firmware flaw that weakened seed generation on COLDCARD Mk4, Mk5 and Q devices. If you created a seed on a COLDCARD before the fixed firmware releases, read the advisory below and Coinkite''s [migration guide](https://coldcard.com/security/migrate) **before doing anything else**. Updating firmware alone does not fix an existing seed.
## Overview
The COLDCARD Q from Canadian company Coinkite is a Bitcoin-only signing device with a full QWERTY keyboard, a colour screen, a QR scanner, NFC, two microSD slots and battery power. It is designed for **air-gapped** use: transactions travel between your computer or phone and the device as partially signed Bitcoin transactions (PSBTs) on microSD, as QR codes or via NFC, so the device never needs a data connection to sign.
## Security architecture
On paper the Q is one of the most security-focused devices available: Bitcoin-only firmware, **two secure elements from different vendors**, air-gapped signing, and advanced features such as duress and "trick" PINs, Seed XOR and extensive multisig controls.
In mid-2026, however, Coinkite disclosed that a **firmware bug weakened the randomness used to generate seeds**. According to Coinkite''s advisory, affected Mk4, Mk5 and Q seeds have roughly 72 bits of entropy instead of the expected 128 — low enough that attackers were able to regenerate private keys offline. Third-party reports attribute significant losses to the flaw. Fixed firmware is available, and later releases add mandatory user-supplied entropy for new seeds.
This matters more than any feature. Generating a secret that nobody else can guess is the single job a hardware wallet cannot get wrong. We have therefore **reduced the COLDCARD Q''s security score substantially** and will revisit it as the fix accumulates a track record and independent analysis is published. (Seeds created with at least 50 private, unrecorded dice rolls are treated separately by Coinkite.)
The firmware source is published, but under a **source-available** licence rather than a standard open-source licence.
## What owners should do
1. Read Coinkite''s official [security status page](https://coldcard.com/security/status).
2. Update to the current firmware for your model.
3. Follow the [migration guide](https://coldcard.com/security/migrate): generate a **new** seed on fixed firmware (optionally adding dice rolls), back it up properly, and move funds to it.
4. For multisig setups, replace any affected COLDCARD key in the quorum.
5. Be alert to scams: no legitimate company will ask for your seed words to "check" or "migrate" them.
## Setup and user experience
The Q is much friendlier than earlier COLDCARDs thanks to its keyboard and larger screen, but it remains a device for people who want control and are willing to learn PSBT workflows. Pairing it with coordinator software such as Sparrow or Nunchuk is straightforward once you understand the concepts.
## Supported cryptocurrencies
Bitcoin only.
## Backup and recovery
Standard **BIP39** recovery phrases, encrypted **microSD backups**, **Seed XOR** (splitting a seed into parts that can each look like a valid seed) and **passphrases** are all supported. For multisig, COLDCARD''s tooling is among the best available.
## Privacy considerations
Because it signs air-gapped and works with any PSBT-capable wallet, the Q lets you pair it with your own node and privacy-focused software. It does not require an account or a vendor app.
## Pricing and value
The Q is priced at the upper end of Bitcoin-only devices. For experienced users who will use its multisig and air-gap features, the hardware is good value; the security score reflects the 2026 incident, not the price.
## Compared with alternatives
[[table:coldcard-q,blockstream-jade-plus,keystone-3-pro,foundation-passport-prime]]
- **Blockstream Jade Plus** — cheaper, open-source, air-gap via camera, but no secure element.
- **Keystone 3 Pro** — air-gapped QR device with a Bitcoin-only firmware option and multisig support.
- **Foundation Passport Prime** — premium Bitcoin device with camera, tamper detection and a large touchscreen.
See [COLDCARD Q vs BitBox02](/en/compare/coldcard-q-vs-bitbox02).
## Final verdict
The COLDCARD Q''s design and feature set are excellent for advanced Bitcoin users, particularly in multisig. But we cannot rate it as highly as before the 2026 seed-generation flaw. If you already use one, prioritise migration. If you are buying now, insist on fixed firmware before creating a seed, consider dice-roll entropy, and remember that a multisig setup with devices from different vendors protects you against exactly this kind of single-vendor failure.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{coldcard-q}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Coinkite''s advisory says seeds generated on Mk4, Mk5 and Q before the fixed firmware releases are affected, with an exception for seeds created with sufficient private dice rolls. Check Coinkite''s official status and migration pages for the exact versions and steps.", "q": "Is my COLDCARD seed affected?"}, {"a": "No. Updating firmware does not change or repair an existing seed. Affected users need to create a new seed on fixed firmware and move their funds.", "q": "Does updating firmware fix an affected seed?"}, {"a": "New seeds generated on fixed firmware are not affected by this flaw, according to Coinkite. Update firmware before creating a seed, consider adding dice-roll entropy, and verify the firmware version on the device.", "q": "Is it safe to buy a COLDCARD Q now?"}, {"a": "The firmware source code is published, but under a source-available licence rather than a standard open-source licence.", "q": "Is COLDCARD open source?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://coldcard.com/security/status", "label": "COLDCARD — security status"}, {"url": "https://coldcard.com/security/migrate", "label": "COLDCARD — migrate an affected seed"}], "verdict": "The COLDCARD Q remains one of the most capable air-gapped Bitcoin signers, with excellent multisig support and unique security features. But the 2026 seed-generation flaw was a failure in the most fundamental job a hardware wallet has. If you own one, follow Coinkite''s migration guidance now. If you are buying, only use seeds generated on fixed firmware — and weigh alternatives carefully."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.971325+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.852971+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('f6fd8977-c8b4-4be2-ada4-50778593e335', 'comparison', 'en', 'bitbox02-vs-ledger-nano-x', 'cmp-bitbox02-ledger-nano-x', 'BitBox02 vs Ledger Nano X: Open Source or Maximum Compatibility?', 'BitBox02 vs Ledger Nano X (2026): Which Should You Buy?', 'BitBox02 vs Ledger Nano X compared: security architecture, open source, backups, iPhone support, assets and price — with a clear recommendation for each type of user.', 'Two compact wallets with very different philosophies: transparency and simplicity versus breadth and mobility.', '## The short answer
[[table:bitbox02,ledger-nano-x]]
The **BitBox02** is the better fit for Bitcoin-focused users who want open-source software, a Bitcoin-only edition and a straightforward backup. The **Ledger Nano X** is the better fit if you hold many different assets or want to sign on an iPhone.
## Security philosophy
These devices represent the two main schools of hardware-wallet design.
**Ledger** puts everything inside a secure element running its own closed operating system. Secure elements are built to resist physical attacks, and Ledger''s implementation has a long track record. But because the OS is closed, you cannot verify independently what it does — a point underlined in 2023 when Ledger Recover showed that seed material can be exported with user consent. The Nano X also uses an older EAL5+ chip than Ledger''s newer devices.
**BitBox02** runs open-source firmware on a general-purpose microcontroller and uses a secure chip to protect the secrets needed to unlock the wallet and to rate-limit PIN attempts. This gives outsiders the ability to audit the wallet logic, while still adding protection against physical extraction.
If you only hold Bitcoin, the **BitBox02 Bitcoin-only edition** also removes all altcoin code from the device. Ledger offers no Bitcoin-only firmware.
## Connectivity and mobile use
The Nano X has **Bluetooth and a battery**, so it works with Ledger''s app on iPhone and Android. The BitBox02 is **USB-C only** and works with desktop and Android, but not iPhone. Neither device signs air-gapped.
Some people deliberately prefer no radio. Bluetooth on the Nano X carries only encrypted, already-approved data, and keys never leave the secure element — but it is still an additional interface.
## Backups
Both use the standard **24-word BIP39** phrase and support passphrases. The BitBox02 adds an optional **microSD backup**, which many beginners find easier than writing words down correctly. Ledger offers **Ledger Recover**, a paid cloud backup service; whether you consider that a feature or a risk is a personal trust decision.
## Asset support
Ledger supports thousands of coins and tokens. The BitBox02 Multi supports a curated shortlist. If you need a specific altcoin, check before buying.
## Privacy and vendor history
BitBox''s app supports connecting to your own node and Tor. Ledger''s 2020 customer-database breach exposed contact details and led to years of phishing; it did not compromise devices, but it is relevant if privacy matters to you.
## Who should choose which
- **BitBox02:** Bitcoiners, open-source advocates, desktop and Android users, beginners who want the easiest robust backup.
- **Ledger Nano X:** multi-asset holders, iPhone users, people who want the largest ecosystem of compatible apps.
Read the full [BitBox02 review](/en/wallets/bitbox02) or compare more devices in our [comparison tool](/en/compare?w=bitbox02,ledger-nano-x).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,ledger-nano-x}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "They make different trade-offs. Ledger relies on a secure element running closed firmware; BitBox02 combines open-source firmware with a secure chip. Neither is universally more secure — it depends on whether you prioritise physical-attack resistance or auditability, and how you back up.", "q": "Which is more secure, BitBox02 or Ledger Nano X?"}, {"a": "The Ledger Nano X, via Bluetooth. The original BitBox02 does not; the BitBox02 Nova does.", "q": "Which works with iPhone?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": "Choose the BitBox02 if you mainly hold Bitcoin, use desktop or Android, and value open-source firmware and an easy microSD backup. Choose the Ledger Nano X if you need an iPhone-compatible device or broad altcoin support and are comfortable trusting Ledger''s closed secure-element OS."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.973994+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.855083+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('646b7030-2421-4188-a936-806396fbb888', 'comparison', 'en', 'trezor-safe-5-vs-ledger-flex', 'cmp-trezor-safe-5-ledger-flex', 'Trezor vs Ledger: Safe 5 vs Flex Compared', 'Trezor vs Ledger (2026): Trezor Safe 5 vs Ledger Flex Compared', 'Trezor vs Ledger in 2026: we compare the Trezor Safe 5 and Ledger Flex on security model, open source, screens, backups, mobile support and value.', 'The classic rivalry, using each brand''s mid-range touchscreen device.', '## The short answer
[[table:trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex]]
Trezor and Ledger are the two best-known hardware-wallet brands, and they disagree about the most important design question: **should the security-critical code be open source?** Trezor says yes; Ledger relies on a closed secure-element OS. Both now use secure elements, so the gap in physical-attack resistance is much smaller than it was with older Trezor models.
## Security model
- **Trezor Safe 5:** open-source firmware on the main chip, plus a secure element that holds a secret required (with your PIN) to unlock the seed. The secure element''s internal firmware is closed, but it does not run the wallet logic.
- **Ledger Flex:** keys are generated and used inside a certified secure element running Ledger''s closed OS. Strong physical protections, limited independent auditability.
If you want to minimise how much you must trust any single vendor, open source is a meaningful advantage. If you primarily worry about a sophisticated attacker with physical access to your device, Ledger''s all-in-secure-element design is a strength — though a strong passphrase narrows the difference considerably on either device.
## Screens and usability
The Flex''s ~2.8-inch E Ink touchscreen is larger and better suited to reviewing long, complex transactions such as DeFi interactions. The Safe 5''s colour touchscreen is smaller but comfortable for PIN, passphrase and address checks. Both are big improvements on button-driven devices.
## Connectivity
The Flex has USB-C, **Bluetooth and NFC**, so it works with iPhone. The Safe 5 is **USB-only** (Trezor''s Bluetooth model is the Safe 7). Neither signs air-gapped.
## Backups
Both support **BIP39** and **passphrases**. Trezor adds **SLIP-39 multi-share backups** (e.g. 2-of-3), a powerful option for long-term storage. Ledger offers the optional paid **Ledger Recover** service.
## Assets and ecosystem
Ledger supports more coins and has deeper integration with Web3 apps. Trezor covers the major assets and offers a **Bitcoin-only firmware**.
## Vendor history
Ledger''s 2020 customer-data breach and 2023 Connect Kit supply-chain attack did not compromise device keys but affected users. Trezor''s older models were shown to be vulnerable to physical seed extraction, which the Safe series addresses with a secure element.
## Who should choose which
- **Trezor Safe 5:** Bitcoiners, open-source advocates, long-term holders who want multi-share backups.
- **Ledger Flex:** active multi-chain and DeFi users, iPhone users, people who value a large signing screen.
Explore both: [Trezor Safe 5 review](/en/wallets/trezor-safe-5) · [Ledger Flex review](/en/wallets/ledger-flex).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "For Bitcoin-only users, Trezor''s open-source and Bitcoin-only firmware is usually the better fit. Ledger works well for Bitcoin too, especially with Sparrow, but has no Bitcoin-only firmware.", "q": "Is Trezor or Ledger better for Bitcoin?"}, {"a": "Ledger generally supports more assets and its large screens help with clear signing of complex transactions.", "q": "Is Trezor or Ledger better for altcoins and DeFi?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": "Pick the Trezor Safe 5 if open-source firmware, multi-share backups and a Bitcoin-only option matter to you. Pick the Ledger Flex if you are a multi-chain user who wants a big screen, Bluetooth and NFC, and you accept Ledger''s closed secure-element OS."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.975198+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.855993+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('c76069a7-6744-4aa0-ae9e-acf104f2e974', 'comparison', 'en', 'coldcard-q-vs-bitbox02', 'cmp-coldcard-q-bitbox02', 'COLDCARD Q vs BitBox02: Advanced Air-Gap or Effortless Simplicity?', 'COLDCARD Q vs BitBox02 (2026): Bitcoin Wallets Compared', 'COLDCARD Q vs BitBox02 Bitcoin-only: air-gapped signing vs simplicity, the 2026 COLDCARD seed flaw, backups, multisig and which suits you.', 'Both are favourites with Bitcoiners — for very different kinds of users.', '## The short answer
[[table:coldcard-q,bitbox02]]
These are two of the most popular Bitcoin-focused devices, and they could hardly be more different. The **COLDCARD Q** is a full-keyboard, air-gapped power tool. The **BitBox02** (in its Bitcoin-only edition) is a minimalist USB device built to make good security easy.
## The 2026 COLDCARD seed flaw
In mid-2026 Coinkite disclosed that a firmware bug weakened seed generation on COLDCARD Mk4, Mk5 and Q devices. Seeds generated before the fixed firmware are affected; updating firmware does not fix them, and owners must migrate funds to a new seed. See the [COLDCARD Q review](/en/wallets/coldcard-q) for details. This is the main reason the BitBox02 currently scores higher on security architecture in our model.
## Air-gapped vs connected signing
The COLDCARD Q never needs a data connection: transactions move via **microSD, QR codes or NFC**. The BitBox02 signs over **USB-C**. Air-gapping removes a class of attacks that rely on a malicious computer talking to the device — but well-designed connected wallets mitigate those by showing every detail on the device screen for confirmation.
## Ease of use
The BitBox02 is far easier to set up and use day to day. The COLDCARD Q is friendlier than older COLDCARDs thanks to its keyboard and screen, but PSBT workflows and the sheer number of options make it an expert''s tool.
## Backup options
- **BitBox02:** microSD backup and/or BIP39 words; passphrase.
- **COLDCARD Q:** BIP39 words, encrypted microSD backups, **Seed XOR**, passphrase, dice-roll entropy.
## Multisig
Both are strong multisig signers. COLDCARD''s multisig tooling is more extensive; BitBox02 integrates cleanly with Sparrow and Specter. Combining devices from **different manufacturers** in a multisig is a good defence against exactly the kind of single-vendor failure the 2026 flaw represents.
## Open source
The BitBox02''s firmware and hardware designs are open source. COLDCARD''s firmware is published under a source-available licence.
## Who should choose which
- **BitBox02 (Bitcoin-only):** beginners and most single-signature Bitcoin holders on desktop or Android.
- **COLDCARD Q:** experienced Bitcoiners who want air-gapped signing and advanced controls, and who will follow Coinkite''s migration guidance carefully.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{coldcard-q,bitbox02}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Not necessarily. The flaw affects seeds generated on older firmware; Coinkite has released fixes. What you must do is migrate any affected seed. Whether you also change vendor depends on your trust in Coinkite''s remediation.", "q": "Should I switch from COLDCARD to BitBox02 after the 2026 flaw?"}, {"a": "Yes. Both work as signers in a Bitcoin multisig with coordinators such as Sparrow or Nunchuk — using different vendors reduces single-vendor risk.", "q": "Can I use both together?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": "For most Bitcoin holders, especially beginners, the BitBox02 Bitcoin-only edition is the simpler and currently more conservative choice. The COLDCARD Q suits experienced users who need air-gapped signing and advanced multisig features — provided they use seeds generated on fixed firmware after the 2026 entropy flaw."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.976053+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.856821+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('13859880-e297-476e-9e0f-1aa2e4ff9816', 'comparison', 'en', 'blockstream-jade-plus-vs-trezor-safe-3', 'cmp-jade-plus-trezor-safe-3', 'Blockstream Jade Plus vs Trezor Safe 3: Which Open-Source Wallet?', 'Jade Plus vs Trezor Safe 3 (2026): Open-Source Wallets Compared', 'Jade vs Trezor: we compare the Blockstream Jade Plus and Trezor Safe 3 on security model, air-gapped signing, backups, mobile support and price.', 'Two open-source devices, two different answers to the secure-element question.', '## The short answer
[[table:blockstream-jade-plus,trezor-safe-3]]
Both devices publish their firmware as open source and both are reasonably priced. The difference lies in how they protect keys against physical attacks, and in how you connect them.
## Protecting the seed
- **Trezor Safe 3:** a **secure element** stores a secret that, together with your PIN, is needed to decrypt the seed.
- **Jade Plus:** no secure element. A **blind oracle** (PIN server) holds a secret required to unlock the encrypted seed; it never sees your keys, and you can run it yourself.
Both designs aim to make it impractical for a thief to extract your seed. They rely on different assumptions; adding a strong passphrase improves either.
## Connectivity and air-gap
The Safe 3 is **USB-only**. The Jade Plus offers **USB, Bluetooth and a camera** for QR-based, fully air-gapped signing — and works with iPhone. If air-gapped signing or iPhone support matters to you, that settles it.
## Backups
The Safe 3 supports **multi-share SLIP-39 backups** in addition to BIP39. The Jade Plus supports BIP39 and **SeedQR**, which enables "stateless" use where the seed is never stored on the device.
## Assets
The Safe 3 supports Bitcoin and many other assets (with an optional Bitcoin-only firmware). The Jade Plus supports Bitcoin and Liquid only.
## Who should choose which
- **Trezor Safe 3:** beginners on a budget, users who want a secure element and multi-asset support, long-term holders wanting multi-share backups.
- **Jade Plus:** Bitcoiners who want mobile convenience and the option of air-gapped QR signing, and anyone building a multi-vendor multisig.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{blockstream-jade-plus,trezor-safe-3}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "It is a different trade-off. Jade uses a PIN server (''blind oracle'') instead. For larger amounts, a strong passphrase or stateless SeedQR operation substantially mitigates physical-access risks.", "q": "Is the lack of a secure element in Jade a problem?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": "The Trezor Safe 3 is the simpler, cheaper choice with a secure element and multi-asset support. The Jade Plus is the better choice for Bitcoiners who want mobile use over Bluetooth and the option of fully air-gapped QR signing, and who understand its blind-oracle security model."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.976784+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.857553+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('9dc16671-25d1-4eb0-ac91-eb8a2a5e2611', 'comparison', 'en', 'trezor-safe-3-vs-ledger-nano-s-plus', 'cmp-trezor-safe-3-ledger-nano-s-plus', 'Trezor Safe 3 vs Ledger Nano S Plus: The Best Budget Hardware Wallet?', 'Trezor Safe 3 vs Ledger Nano S Plus (2026): Budget Wallets Compared', 'Trezor Safe 3 vs Ledger Nano S Plus: two low-cost hardware wallets compared on open source, secure element, backups and asset support.', 'The two most popular entry-level devices, side by side.', '## The short answer
[[table:trezor-safe-3,ledger-nano-s-plus]]
If you are buying your first hardware wallet on a budget, these are the two most likely candidates. Both have secure elements, both are USB-only, and both cost well under €/£100 at their recommended prices.
## Open source vs closed secure element
The **Safe 3** runs open-source firmware and uses its secure element to guard a secret needed to unlock the seed. The **Nano S Plus** keeps everything in a secure element running Ledger''s closed OS. Both protect against casual physical attacks; they differ in how much you must trust the vendor.
## Backups
The Safe 3 supports **multi-share backups** (SLIP-39) as well as standard BIP39 words. The Nano S Plus uses BIP39 words, with Ledger Recover available as a paid option.
## Assets
The Nano S Plus supports more assets. The Safe 3 covers the major ones and offers a **Bitcoin-only firmware**.
## Usability
Both use two buttons and a small monochrome screen — fine for occasional use, slower for checking long addresses.
## Who should choose which
- **Trezor Safe 3:** Bitcoin-focused beginners, open-source advocates, anyone wanting multi-share backups.
- **Ledger Nano S Plus:** beginners with a broad altcoin portfolio.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{trezor-safe-3,ledger-nano-s-plus}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": "Both are excellent value. Choose the Trezor Safe 3 for open-source firmware, multi-share backups and a Bitcoin-only option. Choose the Ledger Nano S Plus for the widest asset support at a low price."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.977643+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.858336+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('7dcced60-72cd-45aa-833e-447555370c45', 'guide', 'en', 'best-bitcoin-hardware-wallets', 'guide-best-bitcoin', 'The Best Bitcoin Hardware Wallets (Bitcoin-Only Picks)', 'Best Bitcoin Hardware Wallets 2026: Bitcoin-Only Devices Compared', 'The best Bitcoin-only hardware wallets and Bitcoin-only firmware options in 2026, compared on security, air-gap, multisig and ease of use.', 'Devices and firmware editions built for Bitcoin alone — and why a smaller codebase matters.', 'If you only hold Bitcoin, a **Bitcoin-only** device or firmware is worth considering: every line of altcoin code removed is one less place for a bug to hide. Several manufacturers build Bitcoin-only devices, and others offer Bitcoin-only firmware editions of their multi-coin hardware.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,blockstream-jade-plus,trezor-safe-5,foundation-passport-prime]]
## Why these picks
**BitBox02 (Bitcoin-only edition).** The simplest robust choice for most Bitcoiners: open source, secure chip, microSD backup, good multisig support.
**Blockstream Jade Plus.** Bitcoin and Liquid only, open source, with camera-based air-gapped signing and iPhone support at a mid-range price. No conventional secure element.
**Trezor Safe 5 (Bitcoin-only firmware).** Trezor''s open-source model with a touchscreen and multi-share backups, which suit long-term storage.
**Foundation Passport Prime.** A premium, phone-like Bitcoin device with a large touchscreen, camera and tamper detection. Expensive, and a young platform.
**COLDCARD Q.** The most feature-rich air-gapped Bitcoin signer, with excellent multisig tooling. Because of the 2026 seed-generation flaw it scores lower on security in our model; use seeds generated on fixed firmware only.
## Taking it further
For larger amounts, consider a **multisig** setup that combines devices from different manufacturers, so a flaw in one vendor''s device cannot cost you your funds. Start with our [multisig guide](/en/learn/multisig-custody) and see [best wallets for multisig](/en/best/best-hardware-wallets-for-long-term-bitcoin-storage).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,blockstream-jade-plus,trezor-safe-5,foundation-passport-prime,coldcard-q}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Bitcoin-only firmware contains no code for other blockchains, which reduces the attack surface and the number of updates that could introduce bugs.", "q": "Why choose a Bitcoin-only wallet?"}, {"a": "Not on Bitcoin-only firmware. Some devices (BitBox02, Trezor) let you choose a different edition or firmware; dedicated Bitcoin-only devices cannot.", "q": "Can I add altcoins later?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Best for most Bitcoiners (Bitcoin-only edition)", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Best value with air-gap option", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}, {"label": "Best with multi-share backups (Bitcoin-only firmware)", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Best premium Bitcoin device", "product": "foundation-passport-prime"}, {"label": "Most advanced features (read the 2026 advisory)", "product": "coldcard-q"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.979485+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.860002+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('3c520330-0cd2-4f92-bd7c-8fab4ffe1b76', 'guide', 'en', 'best-hardware-wallets-for-beginners', 'guide-best-beginners', 'The Best Hardware Wallets for Beginners', 'Best Crypto Hardware Wallets for Beginners (2026)', 'The best beginner-friendly hardware wallets in 2026: simple setup, clear screens, safe backups — plus the mistakes new users should avoid.', 'Easy to set up, hard to get wrong.', 'For a first hardware wallet, **ease of getting the basics right** matters more than advanced features. The best beginner devices guide you through creating and backing up your seed, show transaction details clearly, and don''t overwhelm you with options.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-nano-s-plus]]
## Why these picks
**BitBox02.** Clear setup through the BitBoxApp and an optional microSD backup make it hard to get wrong. The Bitcoin-only edition is ideal if you are starting with Bitcoin.
**Trezor Safe 3.** A secure element, open source and excellent backup options at an entry-level price.
**Trezor Safe 5.** If you can spend more, the touchscreen makes everything easier.
**Ledger Nano S Plus.** The cheapest way to support a wide range of assets with a secure element.
**Tangem.** The simplest experience on a phone — but with no screen, you rely on the app to show what you are signing. Fine for modest amounts; for savings, choose a device with a screen.
## Five beginner rules
1. Buy direct from the manufacturer or an authorised reseller.
2. Set it up yourself — never use a device that arrives with words already filled in.
3. Write your recovery phrase on paper or metal. Never photograph it or type it anywhere.
4. Send a small test amount and practise a recovery before moving larger sums.
5. Nobody legitimate will ever ask for your recovery phrase.
Continue with [what is a hardware wallet?](/en/learn/what-is-a-hardware-wallet) and [seed phrases explained](/en/learn/seed-phrases-and-recovery).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-nano-s-plus,tangem-wallet}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Storing the recovery phrase digitally — in a photo, a notes app, cloud storage or email. Write it down on paper or metal and keep it offline.", "q": "What''s the biggest beginner mistake?"}, {"a": "It is very simple, but it has no screen, so you cannot verify transactions on the device. We consider it suitable for modest amounts; for savings, choose a device with a screen.", "q": "Is a card wallet like Tangem safe for beginners?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Best for beginners overall", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Best on a budget", "product": "trezor-safe-3"}, {"label": "Most comfortable to use", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Best budget multi-asset", "product": "ledger-nano-s-plus"}, {"label": "Simplest for small amounts on mobile", "product": "tangem-wallet"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.98054+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.861917+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('034c0246-05c1-4d26-bee9-9ea3e3a2696c', 'guide', 'en', 'best-air-gapped-hardware-wallets', 'guide-best-airgapped', 'The Best Air-Gapped Hardware Wallets', 'Best Air-Gapped Hardware Wallets 2026: QR & microSD Signing', 'The best air-gapped hardware wallets in 2026 for QR-code and microSD signing: Keystone, Jade Plus, Passport Prime, COLDCARD, SafePal and NGRAVE compared.', 'Sign transactions without ever connecting the device to a computer.', 'An **air-gapped** hardware wallet never needs a data connection to your computer or phone. Unsigned transactions go to the device as QR codes or on a microSD card; signed transactions come back the same way. Read [air-gapped signing explained](/en/learn/air-gapped-signing) for the details and trade-offs.
[[picks]]
[[table:keystone-3-pro,blockstream-jade-plus,foundation-passport-prime,safepal-s1-pro]]
## Why these picks
**Keystone 3 Pro.** QR-only signing, a large touchscreen, multiple secure elements and a Bitcoin-only firmware option make it the most well-rounded air-gapped device for multi-chain users.
**Blockstream Jade Plus.** Open source with a camera for QR signing at a mid-range price; also works over USB and Bluetooth when convenience matters more.
**Foundation Passport Prime.** A premium Bitcoin device with a camera for air-gapped use, tamper detection and a big screen.
**COLDCARD Q.** Air-gapped by design with microSD, QR and NFC options and superb multisig features — but rated lower after the 2026 seed-generation flaw.
**SafePal S1 Pro.** A low-cost, QR-only device for multi-chain users; closed source and tied to SafePal''s app.
We also profile the **NGRAVE ZERO**, a premium fully air-gapped device; its high price and closed design keep it out of our top picks.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{keystone-3-pro,blockstream-jade-plus,foundation-passport-prime,coldcard-q,safepal-s1-pro,ngrave-zero}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "It removes the data connection between device and computer, eliminating attacks that rely on it. But a well-designed USB wallet that shows everything on its screen is also very secure; air-gapping is one layer, not a guarantee.", "q": "Is air-gapped signing more secure?"}, {"a": "QR is faster and needs no extra media; microSD handles large multisig transactions more easily. Some devices support both.", "q": "QR or microSD?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Best air-gapped overall (multi-chain)", "product": "keystone-3-pro"}, {"label": "Best value for Bitcoin", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}, {"label": "Best premium Bitcoin device", "product": "foundation-passport-prime"}, {"label": "Most advanced (read the 2026 advisory)", "product": "coldcard-q"}, {"label": "Cheapest fully air-gapped", "product": "safepal-s1-pro"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.981382+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.863325+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('49a05a14-4450-4b06-b495-8e7619a2df04', 'guide', 'de', 'beste-hardware-wallets-deutschland', 'guide-best-germany', 'Die besten Hardware Wallets in Deutschland', 'Beste Hardware Wallet 2026: Empfehlungen für Deutschland', 'Die besten Hardware Wallets für Käufer in Deutschland 2026: Empfehlungen nach Bedarf, Euro-Preise, Gewährleistung, Steuern und sicherer Kauf.', 'Unsere Empfehlungen für Käufer in Deutschland – mit Tipps zum sicheren Kauf in der EU.', 'Für Käufer in Deutschland kommen die beliebtesten Geräte von Herstellern aus der EU (Trezor aus Tschechien, Ledger aus Frankreich) und der Schweiz (BitBox). Beim Kauf innerhalb der EU fallen in der Regel keine Einfuhrabgaben an.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex]]
## Warum diese Empfehlungen
**BitBox02.** Quelloffen, mit Sicherheitschip, Bitcoin-only-Edition und dem wohl einfachsten robusten Backup (microSD plus Standard-Wörter). Für iPhone-Nutzer gibt es die BitBox02 Nova.
**Trezor Safe 3.** Secure Element, Open Source und Multi-Share-Backup zum Einstiegspreis – vom EU-Hersteller aus Prag.
**Trezor Safe 5.** Gleiches Sicherheitsmodell mit komfortablem Farb-Touchscreen.
**Ledger Flex.** Großer E-Ink-Bildschirm und sehr breite Asset-Unterstützung; das Betriebssystem im Secure Element ist jedoch Closed Source.
**Blockstream Jade Plus.** Quelloffen, mit Kamera für air-gapped Signieren per QR und iPhone-Unterstützung. Kein klassisches Secure Element.
## Sicher kaufen in Deutschland
- **Direkt beim Hersteller oder autorisierten Händlern kaufen.** Marktplätze und Gebrauchtgeräte meiden.
- **EU-Händler:** 14-tägiges Widerrufsrecht beim Online-Kauf und zweijährige gesetzliche Gewährleistung. Manche Händler schließen geöffnete Sicherheitsgeräte vom Widerruf aus – vorher prüfen.
- **Schweizer und Nicht-EU-Händler:** Es können Einfuhrumsatzsteuer und Bearbeitungsgebühren anfallen.
- **Lieferadresse:** Nach Datenlecks bei Onlineshops der Branche nutzen manche Käufer eine Packstation oder Geschäftsadresse.
## Steuerlicher Hinweis
Gewinne aus dem Verkauf privat gehaltener Kryptowerte sind nach einer Haltefrist von mehr als einem Jahr grundsätzlich steuerfrei (§ 23 EStG). Eine Hardware Wallet ändert daran nichts, aber dokumentieren Sie Ihre Transaktionen. Keine Steuerberatung.
Neu im Thema? Lesen Sie [Was ist eine Hardware Wallet?](/de/learn/was-ist-eine-hardware-wallet) oder nutzen Sie den [Wallet-Finder](/de/quiz).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex,blockstream-jade-plus}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Das hängt von Ihrem Bedrohungsmodell und Ihrem Backup ab. Unsere Wertung gewichtet die Sicherheitsarchitektur am stärksten, doch ein gut gesichertes Mittelklassegerät ist sicherer als ein Premiumgerät mit schlechtem Backup.", "q": "Welche Hardware Wallet ist die sicherste?"}, {"a": "Gewinne aus privaten Veräußerungsgeschäften mit Kryptowerten sind in Deutschland nach einer Haltefrist von mehr als einem Jahr grundsätzlich steuerfrei (§ 23 EStG). Dies ist keine Steuerberatung.", "q": "Muss ich Gewinne aus Bitcoin versteuern?"}, {"a": "Beim Online-Kauf in der EU gilt grundsätzlich ein 14-tägiges Widerrufsrecht. Manche Händler schließen geöffnete Sicherheitsprodukte aus – prüfen Sie die Bedingungen vor dem Kauf.", "q": "Kann ich eine geöffnete Hardware Wallet zurückgeben?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Beste Wahl insgesamt", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Bestes Preis-Leistungs-Verhältnis (EU-Hersteller)", "product": "trezor-safe-3"}, {"label": "Bester Open-Source-Touchscreen", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Beste Multi-Chain-Wallet (EU-Hersteller)", "product": "ledger-flex"}, {"label": "Beste Air-Gap-Option für Bitcoin", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.984235+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.866355+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('9ec1910f-149a-4cfd-a787-71f6aaa20c6e', 'guide', 'en', 'best-hardware-wallets-for-long-term-bitcoin-storage', 'guide-best-longterm-multisig', 'Best Hardware Wallets for Long-Term Bitcoin Storage and Multisig', 'Best Wallets for Long-Term Bitcoin Storage & Multisig (2026)', 'The best hardware wallets for long-term Bitcoin cold storage and multisig in 2026: devices that work well together, backup strategy and inheritance considerations.', 'For savings measured in years: strong backups, multisig compatibility and vendor diversity.', 'Long-term storage is less about the "best" device and more about a **setup** that survives device failure, vendor failure, loss, theft and your own absence. Two approaches dominate:
1. **Single-signature with a robust backup** — e.g. a multi-share (SLIP-39) backup spread across locations, or a seed plus a passphrase stored separately.
2. **Multisignature (e.g. 2-of-3)** — three keys on devices from **different manufacturers**, any two of which can spend.
The 2026 COLDCARD seed-generation flaw is a reminder of why vendor diversity matters: in a 2-of-3 multisig with three different vendors, a flaw in one device''s key generation does not put funds at risk on its own.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-5,blockstream-jade-plus,keystone-3-pro]]
## Building a 2-of-3 multisig
- Choose a coordinator: **Sparrow** (desktop) or **Nunchuk** (mobile/desktop) are popular.
- Use three devices from different vendors, e.g. BitBox02 + Jade Plus + Keystone 3 Pro.
- Back up **each** seed and the **wallet descriptor** (the multisig configuration). Without the descriptor, recovering a multisig is much harder.
- Store keys in different places so no single location holds two keys.
- Practise a recovery before depositing significant funds.
Read [multisig custody explained](/en/learn/multisig-custody) and [Bitcoin inheritance planning](/en/learn/bitcoin-inheritance-planning).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-5,blockstream-jade-plus,keystone-3-pro,coldcard-q}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [{"label": "Excellent multisig signer, simple to operate", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Multi-share backups for single-sig storage", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Open-source, air-gap-capable second vendor", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}, {"label": "Air-gapped QR signer for multisig", "product": "keystone-3-pro"}, {"label": "Advanced multisig tooling (fixed firmware only)", "product": "coldcard-q"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.985463+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.867416+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('4f7c6db0-6245-497c-9715-54c2716cca3d', 'learn', 'en', 'what-is-a-hardware-wallet', 'learn-what-is-hw', 'What Is a Hardware Wallet?', 'What Is a Hardware Wallet? A Plain-English Guide', 'What a hardware wallet is, how it protects your Bitcoin and crypto, what it can''t protect against, and whether you need one.', 'A small device that keeps your private keys offline and signs transactions — explained without jargon.', 'A **hardware wallet** is a small, dedicated device that stores the **private keys** that control your Bitcoin or other crypto assets, and uses them to **sign transactions** without ever exposing them to your computer or phone.
## Your coins aren''t "in" the device
Bitcoin lives on the blockchain — a public ledger. What you own is the ability to move it, and that ability comes from a private key (see [how Bitcoin private keys work](/en/learn/how-bitcoin-private-keys-work)). A hardware wallet keeps that key in a protected environment and only uses it after you physically confirm an action on the device.
## How it protects you
When you send Bitcoin, your wallet app on a computer or phone builds an unsigned transaction and passes it to the hardware wallet. The device displays the destination address and amount on its **own screen**. You check them and confirm with a button or touch. The device signs the transaction internally and returns only the signature. Even if your computer has malware, it never sees your key — and it can''t change the transaction without that change showing on the device screen.
## What it can''t protect against
- **Losing your backup.** If the device breaks and you have no recovery phrase, your funds are gone.
- **Revealing your recovery phrase.** Anyone with those words can take your coins — no device needed.
- **Approving the wrong thing.** If you don''t read the screen and confirm a malicious address, the device will sign it.
- **A compromised device.** Buy only from the manufacturer or an authorised reseller ([how to buy safely](/en/learn/how-to-buy-and-verify-a-hardware-wallet)).
## Do you need one?
If you hold an amount of crypto that you couldn''t afford to lose, and you want to hold it yourself rather than on an exchange, a hardware wallet is the standard tool. Compare [hot and cold wallets](/en/learn/hot-vs-cold-wallets), then try the [wallet finder](/en/quiz) or browse our [best hardware wallets](/en/best/best-hardware-wallets).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "No. Coins exist on the blockchain. The device stores the private keys that let you spend them.", "q": "Are my coins stored on the hardware wallet?"}, {"a": "Nothing is lost as long as you have your recovery phrase. You can restore your wallet on a new device of the same or another compatible brand.", "q": "What happens if my hardware wallet breaks?"}, {"a": "It is difficult if you set it up properly. Most thefts happen when people reveal their recovery phrase to scammers, store it digitally, or approve a malicious transaction without reading the device screen.", "q": "Can Bitcoin be stolen from a hardware wallet?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.986404+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.868388+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('17789e08-b7c8-40c7-a987-60c35bb21f68', 'learn', 'en', 'how-bitcoin-private-keys-work', 'learn-private-keys', 'How Bitcoin Private Keys Work', NULL, 'A clear explanation of Bitcoin private keys, public keys, addresses and signatures — and why randomness matters so much.', 'From a random number to a signature: the cryptography behind self-custody.', '## A key is a very large random number
A Bitcoin **private key** is, at heart, a 256-bit number chosen at random. The number of possible keys is so vast that guessing one is not feasible — **as long as the key was generated with enough randomness**.
## From private key to address
From the private key, wallet software derives a **public key** using elliptic-curve cryptography (secp256k1). This is a one-way operation: easy to compute, practically impossible to reverse. From the public key, an **address** is derived, which you share to receive funds.
## Signatures
To spend, you create a **digital signature** with your private key over the transaction. Anyone can verify the signature with your public key, but nobody can create one without the private key. A hardware wallet''s job is to create these signatures without letting the private key leave the device.
## One seed, many keys
Modern wallets don''t store individual keys. They generate one **seed** — usually represented as 12 or 24 words ([seed phrases explained](/en/learn/seed-phrases-and-recovery)) — and derive all keys from it deterministically (BIP32/BIP39). Back up the seed once and you can recreate every key.
## Why randomness is everything
If the random number behind a seed is predictable, an attacker can reproduce it. In 2026, COLDCARD disclosed a firmware flaw that weakened seed generation on some devices — affected seeds needed to be replaced, even though no device was "hacked" in the usual sense ([details](/en/wallets/coldcard-q)). This is why many advanced users add their own entropy, for example by rolling dice, when generating a seed — and why we weight a vendor''s key-generation track record heavily in our [methodology](/en/methodology).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.988885+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.870891+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('d293a063-750f-4ff0-81bd-aacd51cebe9f', 'learn', 'en', 'seed-phrases-and-recovery', 'learn-seed-phrases', 'Seed Phrases and Recovery, Explained', 'Seed Phrases Explained: What They Are and How Recovery Works', 'What a seed phrase (recovery phrase) is, how BIP39 works, how wallet recovery works, and the mistakes that lead to lost Bitcoin.', 'Those 12 or 24 words are your wallet. Here''s how they work.', 'A **seed phrase** (also called a recovery phrase or mnemonic) is a list of 12 or 24 words that encodes the random seed from which all your wallet''s keys are derived. It is your wallet in human-readable form.
## How BIP39 works
The BIP39 standard maps random bits to words from a fixed list of 2,048 words. Twelve words encode 128 bits of randomness (plus a checksum); 24 words encode 256 bits. Because the list and the derivation method are standardised, the same words recreate the same wallet in any compatible software or device.
## How recovery works
If your device is lost, stolen or broken, you take a new device (or compatible software), choose "restore", and enter your words. The wallet derives the same keys and finds your funds on the blockchain. Your coins were never on the device in the first place.
This is also why the seed phrase is so dangerous in the wrong hands: **anyone who sees it can do exactly the same**.
## Rules that prevent most losses
1. **Never store it digitally.** No photos, notes apps, cloud storage, password managers or emails.
2. **Never type it into a website or app** — except when restoring on a hardware wallet''s own screen. "Wallet validation" sites are scams.
3. **Write it carefully** and double-check spelling against the BIP39 word list.
4. **Protect against fire and water** — consider a metal backup.
5. **Test a recovery** before trusting it with significant funds.
6. **Plan for others** — would your family know it exists? See [inheritance planning](/en/learn/bitcoin-inheritance-planning).
## Alternatives and additions
- **Passphrase** — an extra secret that creates a different wallet from the same words ([passphrases explained](/en/learn/passphrases-and-hidden-wallets)).
- **Multi-share backups (SLIP-39)** — split the backup into shares with a threshold.
- **microSD or card backups** — offered by some vendors (BitBox, Tangem); convenient, but understand how they work.
Continue: [how to back up Bitcoin securely](/en/learn/how-to-back-up-bitcoin).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "No. A password protects access to a device or account. A seed phrase is the wallet itself — anyone with it can recreate your keys anywhere.", "q": "Is a seed phrase the same as a password?"}, {"a": "Usually yes, if both support the BIP39 standard and the same derivation paths. Some backup formats, such as SLIP-39 multi-share, are supported by fewer devices.", "q": "Can I recover my wallet on a different brand of device?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.989587+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.871583+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('d951ea1a-c5ee-4e56-982e-87139fbcb006', 'learn', 'de', 'seed-phrase-sicher-aufbewahren', 'learn-backup', 'Seed-Phrase sicher aufbewahren: So sichern Sie Ihre Bitcoin', 'Seed-Phrase sicher aufbewahren – Anleitung 2026', 'So bewahren Sie Ihre Seed-Phrase (Wiederherstellungsphrase) sicher auf: Papier vs. Metall, Aufbewahrungsorte, Passphrase, Multi-Share und typische Fehler.', 'Das Backup ist wichtiger als das Gerät. So machen Sie es richtig.', 'Ihre **Seed-Phrase** (12 oder 24 Wörter) ist Ihre Wallet. Geht das Gerät verloren, stellen Sie damit alles wieder her – und jeder, der sie findet, kann das ebenso. Ein gutes Backup schützt also vor zwei Gefahren gleichzeitig: **Verlust** und **Diebstahl**.
## Die Grundregeln
1. **Nie digital speichern:** keine Fotos, Cloud, Notiz-Apps, Passwortmanager oder E-Mails.
2. **Nie auf Websites eingeben:** Seiten, die Ihre Wallet „validieren“ wollen, sind Betrug.
3. **Sorgfältig notieren** und mit der BIP39-Wortliste abgleichen.
4. **Wiederherstellung testen**, bevor größere Beträge eingehen.
## Papier oder Metall?
Papier ist ein guter Anfang, verträgt aber weder Feuer noch Wasser. Für langfristige Verwahrung empfehlen sich **Metall-Backups** (gestanzte oder gravierte Platten). Achten Sie darauf, dass Sie die Wörter eindeutig und vollständig festhalten.
## Wo aufbewahren?
An einem Ort, der vor Feuer, Wasser und neugierigen Blicken geschützt ist – und getrennt von der Hardware Wallet. Wer mehr Sicherheit will, verteilt das Risiko:
- **Passphrase:** Ein zusätzliches Geheimnis erzeugt eine andere Wallet aus denselben Wörtern. Seed und Passphrase getrennt aufbewahren – wer nur eines findet, kommt nicht an die Coins. Aber: Ohne Passphrase sind auch Sie ausgesperrt.
- **Multi-Share-Backup (SLIP-39):** Das Backup wird in z. B. drei Teile aufgeteilt, von denen zwei zur Wiederherstellung reichen.
- **Multisig:** Mehrere Schlüssel auf Geräten verschiedener Hersteller.
## An die Familie denken
Würden Ihre Angehörigen im Ernstfall wissen, dass es Ihre Bitcoin gibt und wie sie herankommen – ohne dass jemand es zu früh kann? Ein versiegelter Brief mit Anleitung (ohne die Wörter selbst) bei einer Vertrauensperson oder im Testament kann helfen.
## Typische Fehler
- Nur eine einzige Kopie an einem einzigen Ort.
- Seed und Passphrase am selben Ort.
- Nie getestete Wiederherstellung.
- Ein „Support-Mitarbeiter“ fragt nach den Wörtern – das ist immer Betrug.
Weiter: [Was ist eine Hardware Wallet?](/de/learn/was-ist-eine-hardware-wallet) · [Die besten Hardware Wallets in Deutschland](/de/best/beste-hardware-wallets-deutschland)', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Nein. Fotos landen schnell in Cloud-Backups und sind für Schadsoftware erreichbar. Bewahren Sie die Wörter nur offline auf.", "q": "Darf ich ein Foto meiner Seed-Phrase machen?"}, {"a": "Es schützt gut vor Feuer und Einbruch, aber Sie haben nur zu Öffnungszeiten Zugriff, und Dritte könnten Kenntnis erlangen. Viele kombinieren es mit einer Passphrase oder einem Multi-Share-Backup.", "q": "Ist ein Bankschließfach eine gute Idee?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.990572+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.872448+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('63ad103f-2314-42aa-9044-f01442b19bdf', 'learn', 'en', 'how-to-back-up-bitcoin', 'learn-backup', 'How to Back Up Bitcoin Securely (and Store a Seed Phrase Safely)', 'How to Store a Seed Phrase Safely: Bitcoin Backup Guide', 'How to store a seed phrase safely: paper vs metal, where to keep it, passphrases, multi-share backups, multisig and the mistakes to avoid.', 'Your backup matters more than your device. Here''s how to get it right.', 'A good backup protects against two opposite threats at once: **losing access** (fire, flood, forgetting, death) and **someone else gaining access** (theft, snooping, scams). Every backup decision is a balance between them.
## Step 1: Record the words properly
Write the words clearly, in order, and check each against the BIP39 word list. Many devices let you verify the backup during setup — do it.
## Step 2: Choose a durable medium
Paper is fine to start with but vulnerable to fire and water. For long-term storage, a **metal backup** (stamped or engraved plates) survives far more. Whatever you choose, make sure it is unambiguous and complete.
## Step 3: Choose where to keep it
Somewhere protected from fire, water and casual discovery, and **separate from the hardware wallet**. A safe at home, a trusted relative''s home or a safe-deposit box are common options, each with trade-offs (access hours, who might know about it).
## Step 4: Add protection against theft (optional)
- **Passphrase:** store it separately from the seed. A thief who finds one but not the other gets nothing — but if *you* lose the passphrase, the funds are gone too. See [passphrases explained](/en/learn/passphrases-and-hidden-wallets).
- **Multi-share backup (SLIP-39):** e.g. any 2 of 3 shares can recover, so one lost or stolen share is not fatal. Supported by Trezor and some others.
- **Multisig:** keys on several devices from different vendors. See [multisig custody](/en/learn/multisig-custody).
Don''t improvise by cutting a 24-word phrase in half: that weakens it and creates new ways to lose access.
## Step 5: Test recovery
Send a small amount, wipe or use a second device, restore from your backup, and confirm you see the funds. Only then move significant savings.
## Step 6: Plan for the people you leave behind
Write a letter that explains what exists and how to access it — without including the secrets themselves — and keep it with your will or a trusted person. See [inheritance planning](/en/learn/bitcoin-inheritance-planning).
## Common mistakes
- A single copy in a single place.
- Seed and passphrase stored together.
- A recovery that was never tested.
- Trusting anyone who asks for your words "to help". That is always a scam.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "No. Photos sync to cloud services and are reachable by malware. Keep seed words offline only.", "q": "Can I take a photo of my seed phrase?"}, {"a": "Splitting a BIP39 phrase into halves weakens it and can make recovery fragile. Use a proper scheme instead: a passphrase stored separately, SLIP-39 multi-share, or multisig.", "q": "Should I split my seed words between two places?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.99154+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.873488+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('c2368421-4302-4d40-9409-d861c46797d9', 'learn', 'en', 'passphrases-and-hidden-wallets', 'learn-passphrases', 'Passphrases and Hidden Wallets', NULL, 'How a BIP39 passphrase (25th word) works, when to use one, how hidden wallets protect against theft and coercion, and the risks.', 'The optional extra secret that turns one seed into many wallets.', 'A **passphrase** (sometimes called the "25th word") is an optional extra secret combined with your seed phrase to derive a completely different wallet. Same 24 words + different passphrase = different keys and addresses.
## Why use one?
- **Protection if your seed is found.** A thief with your seed but not your passphrase sees only the wallet without a passphrase — which could be empty or hold a small decoy amount.
- **Defence against physical attacks on the device**, especially relevant for devices without a secure element.
- **Plausible deniability.** Different passphrases open different hidden wallets.
## The risks
- **There is no "wrong passphrase" error.** Any passphrase opens *a* wallet. A typo opens an empty wallet, which can be alarming — and if you forget the exact passphrase (including capitalisation and spaces), the funds are unrecoverable.
- **More to back up.** You now need to protect two secrets, stored separately, and make sure your heirs can find both.
## Good practice
- Use a strong passphrase that you also write down and store **separately** from the seed.
- Enter it on the device itself where possible (touchscreen devices make this easy).
- Note the first receive address of the passphrase wallet, so you can confirm you typed it correctly later.
- Test recovery of the passphrase wallet before depositing.
Devices with good on-device passphrase entry include the [Trezor Safe 5](/en/wallets/trezor-safe-5) and the [BitBox02](/en/wallets/bitbox02).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.992402+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.874521+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('24f70e89-11f8-429b-ae1f-ccc20f89c5de', 'learn', 'en', 'secure-elements-explained', 'learn-secure-elements', 'Secure Elements Explained', NULL, 'What a secure element is, how it protects hardware wallets against physical attacks, the open-source debate, and the alternatives.', 'Why some chips resist attackers with lab equipment — and why that is not the whole story.', 'A **secure element (SE)** is a tamper-resistant chip designed to store secrets and perform cryptographic operations even when an attacker has physical access and specialist equipment. The same class of chip is used in bank cards and passports.
## What it protects against
General-purpose microcontrollers can be vulnerable to **fault injection** (glitching the voltage or clock to skip security checks) and **side-channel analysis** (measuring power use or electromagnetic emissions). Researchers have used such techniques to extract seeds from older wallets without secure elements. Secure elements include countermeasures against these attacks.
## Certification levels
You''ll often see **Common Criteria EAL** ratings (e.g. EAL5+, EAL6+). Higher levels indicate more rigorous evaluation of the chip''s design — but a certificate applies to the chip and a defined scope, not to the whole wallet or its firmware. Check what a certificate actually covers.
## The open-source trade-off
Secure-element vendors typically require non-disclosure agreements, so firmware running *inside* the SE usually cannot be published. Manufacturers resolve this differently:
- **Everything in the SE** (Ledger): strongest physical protection, least public auditability.
- **Open-source firmware + SE for specific secrets** (Trezor Safe series, BitBox02): wallet logic is auditable; the SE guards a secret needed to unlock the seed.
- **No SE, alternative protection** (Blockstream Jade''s blind oracle): avoids closed chips entirely, relies on a PIN server and encourages passphrase or stateless use.
- **Auditable secure elements** (e.g. Trezor''s TROPIC01 in the Safe 7): an attempt to have both.
## What it means for you
An SE matters most if your threat model includes someone stealing the device and investing serious effort. A strong **passphrase** narrows the gap considerably on any device. And no chip protects you from revealing your seed phrase to a scammer.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.993003+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.875333+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('2e807748-f091-4315-b602-f7192e959246', 'page', 'en', 'about', 'page-about', 'About ColdCompare', NULL, 'Who we are, why we compare hardware wallets, and how we keep our recommendations independent.', NULL, 'ColdCompare helps people choose and use hardware wallets to hold Bitcoin and other crypto assets themselves — safely, and without having to wade through marketing claims.
## What we do
- **Structured comparisons.** Every wallet has a profile with the same attributes, so you can compare like with like: secure element, open-source status, connectivity, backup methods, multisig support and more.
- **Transparent scores.** Ratings come from a [published, weighted scoring model](/en/methodology). You can see each category score and how it contributes to the total.
- **Dated, sourced facts.** Every attribute is labelled as *independently verified*, a *manufacturer claim*, or *not yet verified*, with the date it was last checked.
- **Education first.** Our [academy](/en/learn) explains seed phrases, passphrases, multisig and inheritance in plain language, because most losses come from set-up and backup mistakes, not from broken devices.
## What we don''t do
- We don''t sell rankings. Manufacturers cannot pay to be included, to move up a list, or to change a score.
- We don''t claim to have tested devices we haven''t tested. Where an assessment is based on documentation and public research rather than hands-on testing, we say so on the page.
- We never ask for your recovery phrase, private keys or passwords. Nobody legitimate ever will.
## How we make money
Some links to manufacturers and retailers are affiliate links: if you buy through them, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Commission rates are never an input to our scores, rankings or the [wallet finder](/en/quiz). Read the full [affiliate disclosure](/en/affiliate-disclosure).
## Who is behind ColdCompare
[[legal]]
## Contact
Spotted an error or an outdated spec? We want to know — see [contact](/en/contact).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.949032+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.834633+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('6b2f79d8-5a0f-4446-a221-f1a836107da2', 'learn', 'en', 'multisig-custody', 'learn-multisig', 'Multisignature (Multisig) Custody Explained', NULL, 'How Bitcoin multisig works, why 2-of-3 with different vendors is popular, what to back up, and common mistakes.', 'Requiring several keys to spend removes single points of failure.', 'A **multisig** wallet requires signatures from several keys to spend — for example **any 2 of 3**. No single device, backup or person can move the funds alone.
## Why people use it
- **No single point of failure.** Lose one key and you can still spend with the others.
- **Theft resistance.** A thief needs two keys from two places.
- **Vendor diversity.** Using devices from different manufacturers means a flaw in one — such as the 2026 COLDCARD seed-generation issue — doesn''t compromise the wallet by itself.
## What you need
- A **coordinator** wallet, such as Sparrow or Nunchuk, that builds the multisig and transactions.
- **Signers**: hardware wallets with good multisig support (see [our multisig picks](/en/best/best-hardware-wallets-for-long-term-bitcoin-storage)).
- A backup of **each seed** and of the **wallet descriptor** (the configuration listing all public keys and the threshold).
## The descriptor matters
In a 2-of-3, two seeds alone are not enough to find your funds — you also need the public information about the third key, which is contained in the descriptor. Store copies of the descriptor with each key backup. It reveals your balance but cannot spend.
## Common mistakes
- Not backing up the descriptor.
- Keeping two keys in the same place.
- Never testing a recovery.
- Building a setup your heirs can''t understand — see [inheritance planning](/en/learn/bitcoin-inheritance-planning).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.994432+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.877113+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('093807d9-8fe5-4708-a267-a751df01980c', 'learn', 'en', 'hardware-wallet-scams', 'learn-scams', 'Common Hardware Wallet Scams (and How to Avoid Them)', NULL, 'The most common hardware wallet scams — fake support, phishing, tampered devices, fake apps and address poisoning — and how to protect yourself.', 'Most losses aren''t hacks — they''re scams. Know the patterns.', 'Hardware wallets are hard to break. That is why criminals target **people** instead. Almost every scam ends with the victim revealing their recovery phrase or approving a transaction they didn''t understand.
## 1. "Support" asking for your recovery phrase
Fake support agents on social media, email, phone or chat ask you to "verify" or "sync" your wallet by entering your words. **No legitimate company will ever ask for your recovery phrase.** Anyone who does is a thief.
## 2. Phishing emails and letters
After e-commerce data leaks, customers have received convincing emails — and even physical letters — claiming their device must be "updated" via a link. Go to the manufacturer''s site by typing the address yourself, and update only through official software.
## 3. Tampered or pre-configured devices
Devices bought second-hand or from marketplaces may be tampered with. A device that arrives with a pre-printed recovery phrase, or asks you to use one provided, is a scam. Buy directly — see [how to buy and verify a hardware wallet](/en/learn/how-to-buy-and-verify-a-hardware-wallet).
## 4. Fake apps and browser extensions
Malicious copies of wallet apps appear in app stores and search ads. Download only from links on the manufacturer''s official website.
## 5. Address poisoning and clipboard malware
Attackers send tiny transactions from addresses that look like ones you use, hoping you copy the wrong one later. Malware can also replace a copied address. **Always verify the full address on your hardware wallet''s screen.**
## 6. Blind signing
In DeFi, malicious contracts can request approvals that let attackers drain tokens. Use devices and apps that show readable details ("clear signing"), and don''t approve what you don''t understand.
## 7. Recovery services
If you''ve lost funds, "recovery experts" who contact you are almost always running a second scam.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.998967+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.878497+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('c2c2270c-0fe1-4b30-97df-b2bed799c7cb', 'learn', 'en', 'how-to-buy-and-verify-a-hardware-wallet', 'learn-buy-verify', 'How to Safely Buy a Hardware Wallet and Verify Its Authenticity', NULL, 'Where to buy a hardware wallet safely, what to check when it arrives, and how to verify firmware and device authenticity.', 'Buying direct, checking the device and verifying firmware — step by step.', '## Where to buy
- **Directly from the manufacturer**, or from an **authorised reseller** listed on the manufacturer''s website.
- **Avoid** marketplaces, auctions and second-hand devices. Even when sold in sealed packaging, you cannot know what happened to them.
## When it arrives
- Check the packaging is intact, but don''t rely on seals alone — attackers can reseal.
- **The device must not contain a pre-set recovery phrase.** It should generate a new seed during setup (or let you import your own). Any included card with words already filled in means: stop.
- A brand-new device usually arrives without firmware or with firmware that must be verified on first connection.
## Verify the device and firmware
Manufacturers use different methods; follow the official instructions:
- **Device attestation:** many devices prove they are genuine by signing a challenge with a factory key (e.g. Ledger''s "genuine check", Trezor''s authenticity check, BitBox''s attestation).
- **Signed firmware:** devices only install firmware signed by the manufacturer. Some vendors also publish reproducible builds so independent people can confirm that the published source matches the released binary.
- **Air-gapped devices** often update via microSD; check the file''s signature or hash as described in the vendor''s documentation.
## Before trusting it with savings
Send a small amount, practise a recovery from your backup, and only then move larger sums. Keep firmware up to date and read vendor security advisories — we list them on every [wallet profile](/en/wallets).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:14.00003+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.879594+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('a4dab45d-b858-4e40-8f13-02fbbe3589a0', 'page', 'en', 'editorial-policy', 'page-editorial-policy', 'Editorial policy', NULL, 'How ColdCompare researches, fact-checks, corrects and separates editorial content from commercial relationships.', NULL, '## Independence
Editorial decisions — what we cover, how we score it and what we recommend — are made by the editorial team alone. Commercial staff and partners have no say. Manufacturers cannot review or approve content before publication.
## Research and fact-checking
1. Specifications are taken from primary sources wherever possible: official documentation, source-code repositories, certification records and published audits.
2. Each attribute is labelled *independently verified*, *manufacturer claim* or *not yet verified*.
3. Before publication, a second editor checks every factual claim against its source using our editorial checklist. Content that has not passed this check stays in draft and is not public.
4. Security advisories and incidents are reported factually, with links to the vendor''s own disclosure where available.
## Hands-on testing
We only describe a device as tested if we have used it ourselves, and we state what was tested. Otherwise, reviews are clearly labelled as desk-research assessments.
## Corrections
If we get something wrong, we fix it promptly and update the "fact-checked" date. Material corrections — for example, a changed recommendation — are noted at the end of the article. Report errors via our [contact page](/en/contact).
## Updates
Hardware wallets change through firmware updates, price changes and new security research. We review every published profile at least quarterly and immediately after a significant security advisory.
## Sponsored content
We do not currently publish sponsored content. If we ever do, it will be clearly labelled "Sponsored", visually separated from editorial content, and never presented as a review or ranking.
## Safety
We will never ask readers for recovery phrases, private keys or passwords, and we will never publish content that encourages entering them anywhere online.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.954824+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.839382+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('5095ba4a-1fa5-4e83-a44a-6bd9685f8a42', 'page', 'de', 'affiliate-disclosure', 'page-affiliate-disclosure', 'Affiliate-Hinweis', NULL, 'Wie Affiliate-Links bei ColdCompare funktionieren und warum sie unsere Rankings nie beeinflussen.', NULL, 'Einige Links auf dieser Website sind Affiliate-Links. Wenn Sie darüber einkaufen, erhalten wir möglicherweise eine Provision – für Sie ohne Mehrkosten. Unsere Testberichte und Empfehlungen bleiben redaktionell unabhängig.
## So funktioniert es
Klicken Sie auf „Zum Shop“ oder „Preis prüfen“, gelangen Sie über eine kurze Weiterleitung auf unserer Domain (`/go/…`) zum Händler. So zählen wir Klicks pro Produkt und Seite – ohne Ihre IP-Adresse zu speichern oder Tracking-Cookies zu setzen – und können Händlerlinks zentral aktualisieren.
Affiliate-Links sind für Suchmaschinen mit `rel="sponsored"` gekennzeichnet. Haben wir mit einem Hersteller (noch) keine Vereinbarung, verlinken wir auf den offiziellen Shop ohne Vergütung. Wir erfinden weder Tracking-Links noch Partnerschaften.
## Was Affiliate-Beziehungen nicht beeinflussen
- Wertungen und Rankings, die unserer [veröffentlichten Methodik](/de/methodology) folgen.
- Den [Wallet-Finder](/de/quiz), der nur Produktmerkmale und redaktionelle Wertungen nutzt.
- Welche Produkte wir behandeln und was wir darüber schreiben – inklusive Sicherheitshinweisen.
## Wohin wir verlinken
Wir verlinken ausschließlich auf offizielle Hersteller-Shops und geprüfte autorisierte Händler. Käufe auf Marktplätzen oder aus zweiter Hand erhöhen das Risiko manipulierter Geräte.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.95778+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.841761+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('ff88c457-d86b-499e-a8fb-bc2c5d9facdf', 'page', 'de', 'terms', 'page-terms', 'Nutzungsbedingungen', NULL, 'Bedingungen für die Nutzung der Website ColdCompare.', NULL, '> **Hinweis für den Betreiber:** Mustertext – vor dem Start anwaltlich prüfen lassen.
## Nur Information, keine Beratung
Die Inhalte dienen der allgemeinen Information und Bildung. Sie sind keine Finanz-, Anlage-, Rechts- oder Steuerberatung. Kryptowährungen sind volatil; Verluste sind möglich. Sicherheitspraktiken, die für eine Person passen, müssen nicht für andere passen. Sie sind für Ihre Entscheidungen sowie den Schutz Ihrer Schlüssel und Backups selbst verantwortlich.
## Richtigkeit
Wir bemühen uns um aktuelle und richtige Informationen und kennzeichnen jede Angabe mit ihrem Prüfstatus. Produkte, Preise, Firmware und Sicherheitsforschung ändern sich; Angaben können veralten. Prüfen Sie stets die offizielle Herstellerdokumentation.
## Links zu Dritten
Wir verlinken auf Hersteller und Händler und sind nicht für deren Produkte, Inhalte oder Bedingungen verantwortlich. Einige Links sind Affiliate-Links – siehe [Affiliate-Hinweis](/de/affiliate-disclosure).
## Ihre Wiederherstellungsphrase
Geben Sie Ihre Wiederherstellungsphrase, privaten Schlüssel oder Wallet-Passwörter niemals auf dieser Website ein und senden Sie sie uns nie. Wir werden nie danach fragen.
## Urheberrecht
Texte, Datensammlungen, Grafiken und Code sind urheberrechtlich geschützt. Links und kurze Zitate mit Quellenangabe sind erlaubt. Produktnamen und Marken gehören ihren Inhabern.
## Haftung
Die Haftung für Vorsatz und grobe Fahrlässigkeit sowie nach zwingenden gesetzlichen Vorschriften bleibt unberührt. Im Übrigen haften wir, soweit gesetzlich zulässig, nicht für Schäden aus dem Vertrauen auf Informationen dieser Website.
## Betreiber
[[legal]]', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.963222+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.846183+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('02fcdb9b-678b-412d-b5f9-46239b98504a', 'review', 'de', 'bitbox02', 'review-bitbox02', 'BitBox02 Test: Einfache Open-Source-Wallet mit Bitcoin-only-Option', 'BitBox02 Test (2026): Sicherheit, Backup & für wen sie sich lohnt', 'Unser BitBox02-Test: quelloffenes Zwei-Chip-Design, microSD-Backup, Bitcoin-only-Edition, Schwächen und die besten Alternativen.', 'Eine kompakte, quelloffene Wallet, die gute Sicherheitsgewohnheiten leicht macht – sofern Sie kein iPhone brauchen.', '## Überblick
Die BitBox02 des Schweizer Unternehmens Shift Crypto ist ein kleines USB-C-Gerät mit berührungsempfindlichen Kanten statt Tasten. Es gibt zwei Editionen: **Multi** mit Bitcoin und einer kuratierten Auswahl weiterer Assets sowie **Bitcoin-only** mit einer auf Bitcoin reduzierten Firmware. Die Hardware ist identisch.
Ihre Stärke ist kein einzelnes Feature, sondern eine Kombination vernünftiger Voreinstellungen: Open-Source-Firmware und -Hardware, ein Sicherheitschip, ein microSD-Backup, das den fehleranfälligsten Teil der Einrichtung entschärft, und eine übersichtliche BitBoxApp.
## Sicherheitsarchitektur
Die BitBox02 setzt auf ein **Zwei-Chip-Design**: Ein Allzweck-Mikrocontroller führt die quelloffene Firmware aus, ein separater Sicherheitschip speichert Geheimnisse zum Entschlüsseln der Wallet und begrenzt PIN-Versuche. So verbindet sie die Prüfbarkeit offener Firmware mit einem Teil des physischen Schutzes eines Secure Elements – ohne sich vollständig auf dessen geschlossenes Betriebssystem zu verlassen.
- **Bitcoin-only-Firmware:** Weniger Code bedeutet weniger mögliche Fehler. Für reine Bitcoin-Halter eine spürbar kleinere Angriffsfläche.
- **Kein Funk:** Die ursprüngliche BitBox02 hat weder Bluetooth noch NFC – alles läuft über USB-C.
Der Kompromiss: Sie ist **nicht air-gapped**. Signiert wird bei Verbindung mit Computer oder Android-Smartphone. Für die meisten Nutzer ist das unproblematisch, da das Gerät alle Details zur Bestätigung anzeigt.
## Einrichtung und Bedienung
Die Einrichtung erfolgt über die BitBoxApp auf Desktop oder Android. Das Gerätepasswort geben Sie direkt an der BitBox02 ein, danach erstellen Sie das Backup. Die Touch-Bedienung erfordert ein paar Minuten Eingewöhnung. Das kleine Display macht das Prüfen langer Adressen etwas mühsamer als bei Touchscreen-Geräten.
## Unterstützte Kryptowährungen
Die Bitcoin-only-Edition unterstützt Bitcoin. Die Multi-Edition ergänzt eine bewusst begrenzte Liste, etwa Ethereum mit ERC-20-Token und Litecoin – prüfen Sie vor dem Kauf die aktuelle Liste des Herstellers. Wer Hunderte Chains benötigt, ist bei Ledger oder Trezor besser aufgehoben.
## Backup und Wiederherstellung
Hier glänzt die BitBox02: Backup auf **microSD-Karte**, die **24-Wörter-Phrase nach BIP39** oder beides. Dank des Standards ist eine Wiederherstellung auch in anderen Wallets möglich. Eine **optionale Passphrase** ermöglicht versteckte Wallets.
Eine microSD-Karte ist bequem, aber ein digitales Medium: sorgfältig lagern, ggf. mehrere Kopien anlegen und für die Langzeitverwahrung zusätzlich die Wörter auf Papier oder Metall sichern. Mehr dazu: [Seed-Phrase sicher aufbewahren](/de/learn/seed-phrase-sicher-aufbewahren).
## Privatsphäre
Die BitBoxApp kann sich mit einem eigenen Bitcoin-Node verbinden und unterstützt Tor. Außerdem funktioniert das Gerät mit unabhängiger Software wie Sparrow und Electrum.
## Preis und Leistung
Zur unverbindlichen Preisempfehlung (Prüfstatus siehe oben) liegt die BitBox02 im Mittelfeld. Sie zahlen für ein durchdachtes Gesamtpaket und ein transparentes Sicherheitsmodell, nicht für ein großes Display oder Funk. Für Open-Source-Käufer ein gutes Angebot.
## Alternativen im Vergleich
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,ledger-nano-s-plus,bitbox02-nova]]
- **Trezor Safe 3:** ähnlicher Open-Source-Ansatz mit Secure Element, günstiger und mit Multi-Share-Backup – aber ohne microSD.
- **Ledger Nano S Plus:** deutlich mehr Assets, aber geschlossenes Betriebssystem.
- **BitBox02 Nova:** gleiches Konzept mit Bluetooth fürs iPhone und besserem Display, zu höherem Preis.
## Fazit
Die BitBox02 macht es leicht, das Richtige zu tun: am Gerät prüfen, gut sichern, offene Software nutzen. Für Desktop- und Android-Nutzer – besonders in der Bitcoin-only-Edition – eine hervorragende erste Hardware Wallet und für Erfahrene ein solider Signer für Single- oder Multisig. iPhone-Nutzer wählen die Nova; wer air-gapped signieren will, schaut sich COLDCARD, Jade oder Keystone an.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Ja. Shift Crypto veröffentlicht Firmware und Hardware-Designdateien, sodass unabhängige Forscher sie prüfen können.", "q": "Ist die BitBox02 Open Source?"}, {"a": "Die ursprüngliche BitBox02 wird per USB verbunden und am iPhone nicht unterstützt. Die BitBox02 Nova ergänzt Bluetooth für iPhone und iPad.", "q": "Funktioniert die BitBox02 mit dem iPhone?"}, {"a": "Nein. Sie können stattdessen oder zusätzlich die 24-Wörter-Wiederherstellungsphrase (BIP39) notieren.", "q": "Brauche ich die microSD-Karte?"}, {"a": "Wenn Sie nur Bitcoin halten, läuft auf der Bitcoin-only-Edition weniger Code – die Angriffsfläche ist kleiner. Multi lohnt sich nur, wenn Sie die weiteren Assets brauchen.", "q": "Bitcoin-only oder Multi?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://bitbox.swiss/bitbox02/", "label": "Shift Crypto — BitBox02"}], "verdict": "Mit der BitBox02 gelingt quelloffene Selbstverwahrung mit Sicherheitschip besonders einfach. microSD-Backup und Bitcoin-only-Edition sind echte Stärken. iPhone-Nutzer greifen zur Nova; wer air-gapped signieren will, schaut sich Alternativen an."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.967822+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.850448+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('47a9cc46-4ce7-4b72-b9c3-5ae4d690e51e', 'review', 'en', 'ledger-flex', 'review-ledger-flex', 'Ledger Flex Review: Big Screen, Broad Support — and the Closed-Source Question', 'Ledger Flex Review (2026): Security, Ledger Recover & Alternatives', 'Ledger Flex review: E Ink touchscreen, secure element, Bluetooth and NFC, Ledger Recover, past incidents and who should (and shouldn''t) buy it.', 'The most practical Ledger for active multi-chain users. Whether it''s right for you depends on how you feel about closed firmware.', '## Overview
The Ledger Flex is a credit-card-sized device with a roughly 2.8-inch E Ink touchscreen, USB-C, Bluetooth and NFC. It sits between the newer Nano Gen5 and the premium Stax in Ledger''s range and shares the same security model as both.
## Security architecture
Ledger''s approach centres on a **certified secure element** that runs Ledger''s own operating system. Keys are generated and used inside the chip, which is designed to resist physical attacks such as fault injection and side-channel analysis. On this axis, Ledger''s architecture is among the strongest available.
The trade-off is **transparency**. The secure-element operating system is closed source, so outsiders cannot independently audit what it does. In 2023, Ledger announced **Ledger Recover**, an optional paid service that can export encrypted shares of your seed to custodians. It is opt-in, but it demonstrated that the firmware can export seed material when the user consents — and many users concluded that, with closed firmware, they must simply trust Ledger''s implementation. That is a legitimate trust decision, and you should make it consciously.
Ledger has also had incidents outside the device itself: a 2020 **e-commerce database breach** that exposed customer contact details and led to sustained phishing, and a 2023 **supply-chain attack on its Connect Kit** JavaScript library that affected third-party web apps. Neither compromised keys on devices — but both are relevant to your threat model. See the advisories section below.
## Setup and user experience
The Flex is where Ledger''s hardware shines. The large E Ink screen shows full addresses and readable transaction details ("clear signing" where supported), which reduces the risk of approving something you didn''t intend — the cause of many real-world losses in DeFi. Setup runs through the Ledger Wallet app (formerly Ledger Live) on desktop or mobile.
## Supported cryptocurrencies
Asset support is Ledger''s biggest advantage: thousands of coins and tokens via Ledger''s app and third-party wallets such as MetaMask, Rabby or Sparrow (for Bitcoin). There is no Bitcoin-only firmware.
## Backup and recovery
Backup is a standard **24-word BIP39 phrase**, so you can recover in other wallets. **Passphrases** are supported. Ledger Recover is available as an optional paid extra.
## Privacy considerations
Ledger''s app uses Ledger''s infrastructure by default. Bitcoin users who care about privacy can pair the Flex with Sparrow and their own node. Given the 2020 data breach, consider using a non-identifying delivery address or buying from an authorised reseller.
## Pricing and value
The Flex costs considerably more than a Nano S Plus, which has the same core security model. You pay for the screen and wireless features — worth it if you sign transactions often, less so for long-term cold storage.
## Compared with alternatives
[[table:ledger-flex,trezor-safe-5,ledger-stax,ledger-nano-gen5]]
- **Trezor Safe 5** — open-source firmware and multi-share backups; smaller screen, USB-only.
- **Ledger Stax** — same security, bigger curved screen, much higher price.
- **Ledger Nano Gen5** — similar features and E Ink touchscreen in a smaller, cheaper package.
Read [Trezor Safe 5 vs Ledger Flex](/en/compare/trezor-safe-5-vs-ledger-flex) for a deeper comparison.
## Final verdict
For multi-chain users who sign transactions regularly, the Ledger Flex is one of the most practical devices available: a strong secure element and a screen that makes verification realistic. If your priority is open-source firmware, Bitcoin-only operation or minimising trust in a single vendor, choose a Trezor, BitBox or Bitcoin-only signer instead.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{ledger-flex}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "No. Ledger Recover is an optional, paid subscription. You can use the Flex with a standard 24-word recovery phrase only.", "q": "Is Ledger Recover required?"}, {"a": "Bluetooth carries only encrypted, already-signed data; private keys stay in the secure element. Still, every radio is extra attack surface — you can disable Bluetooth and use USB.", "q": "Can a Ledger device be hacked through Bluetooth?"}, {"a": "No. The 2020 incident exposed customer contact data from Ledger''s e-commerce database, which led to phishing. The devices and keys were not compromised.", "q": "Were Ledger devices hacked in the data breach?"}, {"a": "They share the same security model. The Stax has a bigger curved screen and wireless charging; the Flex offers better value.", "q": "Flex or Stax?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://www.ledger.com/", "label": "Ledger — official site"}], "verdict": "The Ledger Flex is an excellent signing device for multi-chain users: its large E Ink screen makes clear signing practical and its asset support is unmatched. The trade-off is trust in Ledger''s closed secure-element OS and a history of customer-data incidents. Bitcoin-only and open-source purists should look elsewhere."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.969907+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.852096+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('49525510-36f3-44f1-b9ed-a1d278e691b9', 'review', 'en', 'blockstream-jade-plus', 'review-blockstream-jade-plus', 'Blockstream Jade Plus Review: Affordable, Open-Source and Air-Gap Ready', 'Blockstream Jade Plus Review (2026): Blind Oracle, QR & Value', 'Blockstream Jade Plus review: how its blind-oracle security works without a secure element, QR air-gapped signing, Liquid support and alternatives.', 'Open-source Bitcoin signing with a camera for QR air-gap at a mid-range price — with a security model you should understand first.', '## Overview
The Jade Plus, launched by Blockstream in January 2025, is an open-source Bitcoin and Liquid signer with a colour screen, battery, USB-C, Bluetooth and a built-in camera. It can be used like a conventional hardware wallet over a cable or Bluetooth, or **fully air-gapped** by scanning QR codes. In 2026 Blockstream added a cheaper Jade Core to the range.
## Security architecture
Jade does not have a conventional secure element. Instead it uses a **"blind oracle"** approach: your seed is encrypted on the device, and decrypting it requires both your PIN and a secret held by a **PIN server**. The server never learns your seed or PIN; it simply refuses to help after too many wrong attempts. You can use Blockstream''s server or **run your own**.
The upside is that Jade''s security does not depend on closed secure-element firmware. The downside is that resistance to a well-equipped attacker with physical access works differently from a secure element; using a strong **BIP39 passphrase**, or **stateless** operation with SeedQR (where the seed is not stored on the device at all), substantially mitigates that risk.
According to third-party reports, Blockstream shipped a **security fix in firmware 1.0.37** (November 2025) and added downgrade protection in 1.0.38, with no known exploitation. Keep firmware current.
## Setup and user experience
Setup works with the Blockstream app (formerly Green) on mobile or desktop, or with third-party wallets such as Sparrow and Nunchuk. QR mode takes a little practice but quickly becomes routine. The jog-wheel style navigation is functional rather than luxurious.
## Supported cryptocurrencies
Bitcoin and Liquid Network assets only.
## Backup and recovery
Standard **BIP39** recovery phrases (12 or 24 words), **SeedQR** backups for stateless use, and **passphrase** support. Because the backup is standard, you are not locked in to Blockstream software.
## Privacy considerations
Jade works with your own node via compatible wallets, and air-gapped QR signing keeps the device off any data connection. Self-hosting the PIN server removes reliance on Blockstream''s infrastructure.
## Pricing and value
Recent listings place the Jade Plus in the mid-range (sources disagreed on the current price — check the official store). For an open-source device with QR air-gap capability, it is strong value.
## Compared with alternatives
[[table:blockstream-jade-plus,trezor-safe-3,coldcard-q,keystone-3-pro]]
- **Trezor Safe 3** — has a secure element and multi-share backups, but no air-gap.
- **COLDCARD Q** — more advanced air-gapped Bitcoin features; see the 2026 advisory.
- **Keystone 3 Pro** — air-gapped QR device with secure elements and a large touchscreen.
See [Blockstream Jade Plus vs Trezor Safe 3](/en/compare/blockstream-jade-plus-vs-trezor-safe-3).
## Final verdict
The Jade Plus offers an unusually flexible, open-source package for Bitcoin users, and its air-gap capability at this price is hard to match. Understand the blind-oracle model, consider a passphrase or stateless use for larger amounts, and keep firmware updated — and it is an excellent choice, especially as one key in a multi-vendor multisig.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{blockstream-jade-plus}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "No. Jade uses a ''virtual secure element'': the encrypted seed can only be unlocked with your PIN plus a secret held by a PIN server (Blockstream''s or one you run yourself).", "q": "Does the Jade Plus have a secure element?"}, {"a": "Yes. Blockstream publishes the PIN server software, so you can self-host it instead of relying on Blockstream''s.", "q": "Can I run my own PIN server?"}, {"a": "Yes. In stateless mode with SeedQR, you scan your seed from a QR backup each session and the device does not keep it.", "q": "Can I use Jade without storing the seed on the device?"}, {"a": "No. Jade supports Bitcoin and assets on Blockstream''s Liquid sidechain only.", "q": "Does it support altcoins?"}], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [{"url": "https://blockstream.com/press-releases/2025-01-03-blockstream-launch-jade-plus/", "label": "Blockstream — Jade Plus launch"}, {"url": "https://blockstream.com/jade/jade-comparison/", "label": "Blockstream — Jade comparison"}], "verdict": "The Jade Plus is a flexible, open-source Bitcoin signer that can work over USB, Bluetooth or fully air-gapped via QR codes. Its blind-oracle design avoids closed secure-element firmware, but it is a different trust model from a secure element. A strong choice for Bitcoiners who understand that trade-off and want air-gap options without a premium price."}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.972418+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.853982+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('74bc59f1-9ef4-48ca-a3c4-d9bf486ff5c6', 'guide', 'en', 'best-hardware-wallets', 'guide-best', 'The Best Hardware Wallets in 2026', 'Best Hardware Wallets 2026: Independent, Security-First Picks', 'The best hardware wallets for Bitcoin and crypto in 2026, picked with a transparent scoring model: best overall, for beginners, Bitcoin-only, air-gapped and multi-chain.', 'Our top picks across use cases, with the reasoning — and the caveats — behind each one.', 'There is no single "best" hardware wallet — the right choice depends on what you hold, which devices you use, and how much complexity you are willing to manage. These picks come from our [published scoring model](/en/methodology) plus a judgement about who each device serves best. Not sure where you fit? Try the [wallet finder](/en/quiz).
[[picks]]
## How the picks compare
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex]]
## Why these picks
**BitBox02 — best overall for most people.** It combines open-source firmware, a secure chip, a Bitcoin-only option and the easiest robust backup we know of (microSD plus standard words). Its main gap is iPhone support; iPhone users should consider the BitBox02 Nova or Trezor Safe 7.
**Trezor Safe 3 — best value.** A secure element, open-source firmware and multi-share backups at an entry-level price. Buttons and a small screen are the compromise.
**Trezor Safe 5 — best open-source touchscreen.** The same security model as the Safe 3, with a touchscreen that makes on-device PIN and passphrase entry and address checks more comfortable.
**Ledger Flex — best for multi-chain and DeFi.** A large E Ink screen for reviewing complex transactions and unmatched asset support. You must be comfortable with Ledger''s closed secure-element OS.
**Blockstream Jade Plus — best affordable air-gap option for Bitcoin.** Open source, camera-based QR signing and iPhone support. It has no conventional secure element; understand its blind-oracle model first.
**Keystone 3 Pro — best air-gapped multi-chain.** QR-only signing, a large touchscreen and a Bitcoin-only firmware option.
## What about COLDCARD?
The COLDCARD Q remains an exceptionally capable Bitcoin signer, but after the 2026 seed-generation flaw we are not including it as a headline pick until the fix has a longer track record. Experienced users may still choose it — read our [review](/en/wallets/coldcard-q) and the advisory first.
## Before you buy
- Buy from the manufacturer or an authorised reseller, never second-hand. See [how to buy and verify a hardware wallet](/en/learn/how-to-buy-and-verify-a-hardware-wallet).
- Plan your backup before your device arrives: [how to back up Bitcoin securely](/en/learn/how-to-back-up-bitcoin).
- Never type your recovery phrase into a computer, phone or website.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex,blockstream-jade-plus,keystone-3-pro}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "There is no single answer. Security depends on your threat model, how you back up your seed and whether you use features like passphrases or multisig. Our scores weigh security architecture most heavily, but a well-backed-up mid-range device is safer than a premium device with a poor backup.", "q": "What is the most secure hardware wallet?"}, {"a": "If you hold an amount of crypto you could not afford to lose, a hardware wallet keeps your keys offline and away from malware on your computer or phone.", "q": "Do I need a hardware wallet?"}, {"a": "We combine our published scores with the use case each device suits best. Affiliate commissions are not an input.", "q": "How did you choose these picks?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Best overall for most people", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Best value", "product": "trezor-safe-3"}, {"label": "Best open-source touchscreen", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Best for multi-chain & DeFi", "product": "ledger-flex"}, {"label": "Best affordable air-gap option for Bitcoin", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}, {"label": "Best air-gapped multi-chain", "product": "keystone-3-pro"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.978523+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.859133+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('a679ea3d-621c-4b0a-b90d-ebe9790e4bd7', 'guide', 'en', 'best-hardware-wallets-uk', 'guide-best-uk', 'The Best Hardware Wallets in the UK', 'Best Hardware Wallets UK (2026): Where to Buy & What to Check', 'The best hardware wallets for UK buyers in 2026, with advice on buying from official stores, import charges, consumer rights and avoiding tampered devices.', 'Our picks for UK buyers, plus practical advice on buying safely and what UK consumer law covers.', 'The best devices for UK buyers are the same as anywhere else — but where and how you buy matters. Prices below are shown in GBP when verified; otherwise check the official store.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex]]
## Buying safely in the UK
- **Buy from the official store or an authorised UK reseller.** Avoid marketplaces and second-hand listings, where tampered devices have been sold.
- **Check whether prices include UK VAT.** Some manufacturers ship from the EU, Switzerland or North America. Depending on value and the seller''s VAT arrangements, you may pay VAT or handling fees on delivery.
- **Know your rights.** When buying from a UK business, the Consumer Rights Act 2015 gives you rights if goods are faulty. Buying from abroad, your rights depend on the seller''s location.
- **Protect your delivery address.** After past e-commerce data leaks in the industry, some buyers use a non-home delivery address.
## Tax note
HMRC treats crypto disposals as potentially subject to Capital Gains Tax. A hardware wallet doesn''t change your tax position, but keep records of transfers. This is not tax advice.
New to this? Start with the [beginner''s guide](/en/best/best-hardware-wallets-for-beginners) or the [wallet finder](/en/quiz).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex,blockstream-jade-plus}', '{"faqs": [{"a": "Possibly. Goods shipped into the UK can attract VAT and handling fees depending on value and how the seller handles VAT. Check at checkout whether prices include UK VAT.", "q": "Will I pay import charges buying a hardware wallet from abroad?"}, {"a": "Yes. Owning and using a hardware wallet to self-custody crypto is legal in the UK. Crypto disposals may be subject to Capital Gains Tax — HMRC guidance applies.", "q": "Is it legal to own a hardware wallet in the UK?"}], "picks": [{"label": "Best overall", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Best value", "product": "trezor-safe-3"}, {"label": "Best open-source touchscreen", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Best multi-chain", "product": "ledger-flex"}, {"label": "Best Bitcoin air-gap option", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.982449+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.864485+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('fabab7b0-9c7e-48f7-98b5-932e430047fe', 'guide', 'en', 'best-hardware-wallets-germany', 'guide-best-germany', 'The Best Hardware Wallets in Germany', 'Best Hardware Wallets in Germany (2026): Picks & Buying Advice', 'The best hardware wallets for buyers in Germany in 2026: EU-based options, EUR pricing, consumer rights and safe buying.', 'Our picks for buyers in Germany, with EU-specific buying advice.', 'For buyers in Germany, popular devices come from manufacturers in the EU (Trezor in Czechia, Ledger in France) and Switzerland (BitBox). Buying within the EU generally avoids import charges.
[[picks]]
[[table:bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex]]
## Buying safely in Germany
- **Buy direct or from authorised resellers.** Avoid marketplaces and used devices.
- **EU sellers:** you benefit from the 14-day withdrawal right for online purchases and the statutory two-year warranty (Gewährleistung). Note that some sellers exclude opened security devices from returns — check before buying.
- **Swiss and non-EU sellers:** imports may attract import VAT (Einfuhrumsatzsteuer) and handling fees; check whether the price includes them.
## Tax note
In Germany, gains from selling privately held crypto after a holding period of more than one year are generally tax-free under § 23 EStG; shorter holding periods may be taxable. A hardware wallet doesn''t change this, but keep records. This is not tax advice.
A [German version of this guide](/de/best/beste-hardware-wallets-deutschland) is available.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{bitbox02,trezor-safe-3,trezor-safe-5,ledger-flex,blockstream-jade-plus}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [{"label": "Best overall", "product": "bitbox02"}, {"label": "Best value (EU manufacturer)", "product": "trezor-safe-3"}, {"label": "Best open-source touchscreen", "product": "trezor-safe-5"}, {"label": "Best multi-chain (EU manufacturer)", "product": "ledger-flex"}, {"label": "Best Bitcoin air-gap option", "product": "blockstream-jade-plus"}], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.983372+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.865418+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('d3c0ea18-d3c4-4df0-85ee-7af874cf751e', 'learn', 'en', 'hot-vs-cold-wallets', 'learn-hot-vs-cold', 'Hot Wallets vs Cold Wallets: What''s the Difference?', NULL, 'Hot wallets vs cold wallets explained: how they differ, the risks of each, and how to combine them sensibly.', 'Spending money vs savings: why most people need both.', 'A **hot wallet** keeps private keys on an internet-connected device — a phone app, a browser extension or desktop software. A **cold wallet** keeps keys on a device that is not exposed to the internet, most commonly a hardware wallet.
## Hot wallets
**Pros:** free, convenient, ideal for frequent small payments.
**Cons:** keys live on a general-purpose device that runs many apps and connects to the internet. Malware, phishing and compromised phones are real risks.
## Cold wallets
**Pros:** keys never touch the internet-connected device; every transaction is confirmed on a separate screen.
**Cons:** cost money, take a little more effort, and must be backed up properly.
## Exchanges are neither
Holding coins on an exchange means the exchange holds the keys. You have a claim on the exchange, not the coins themselves. Exchanges can freeze withdrawals, be hacked or fail.
## A sensible setup
Think of it like cash and a bank vault: a hot wallet for spending money, a hardware wallet for savings. Move only what you need to the hot wallet.
Next: [what is a hardware wallet?](/en/learn/what-is-a-hardware-wallet) · [best hardware wallets for beginners](/en/best/best-hardware-wallets-for-beginners)', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.988125+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.870198+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('8a5b776c-1ba1-4334-bf65-c1c06e296b03', 'learn', 'en', 'air-gapped-signing', 'learn-air-gapped', 'Air-Gapped Signing Explained', NULL, 'What air-gapped signing is, how QR and microSD signing work with PSBTs, the benefits and the limits.', 'Signing transactions without a cable, Bluetooth or any data connection.', 'An **air-gapped** hardware wallet never forms a data connection with your computer or phone. Transactions cross the "air gap" as **QR codes** (via a camera) or on a **microSD card**.
## How it works
1. Your wallet software (e.g. Sparrow, Nunchuk, Keystone''s app) creates an unsigned **PSBT** — a partially signed Bitcoin transaction.
2. You transfer it to the device by scanning a QR code or inserting a microSD card.
3. The device shows the details; you verify and sign.
4. The signed transaction returns the same way, and your software broadcasts it.
## Benefits
- Removes the attack surface of a USB or Bluetooth stack.
- Makes it obvious what data enters and leaves the device.
- Works well with multisig, where each signer can be in a different place.
## Limits
- Slower and more steps than USB.
- The device still needs firmware updates, typically via microSD.
- Air-gapping does **not** protect against a bad seed, a leaked backup or approving the wrong address.
Devices: [best air-gapped hardware wallets](/en/best/best-air-gapped-hardware-wallets).', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.993649+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.87626+00');
INSERT INTO public.articles VALUES ('e6f8ef44-963f-41e0-955e-798c9498a157', 'learn', 'en', 'bitcoin-inheritance-planning', 'learn-inheritance', 'Bitcoin Inheritance Planning', NULL, 'How to make sure your Bitcoin can be inherited: letters of instruction, backups, multisig, professional help — without exposing your keys today.', 'If something happened to you tomorrow, could your family access your Bitcoin?', 'Self-custody means nobody can freeze your Bitcoin — and also that nobody can recover it for your family unless you''ve planned ahead. Inheritance planning balances two goals: **your heirs can get access when needed**, and **nobody can get access before then**.
## Start with a letter of instruction
Write down — without including seed words or passphrases:
- What you hold and roughly where (which wallets, which devices).
- Where backups are stored and how many shares or keys exist.
- Which software to use for recovery, and whom to ask for technical help.
Keep it with your will or a trusted person, and update it when your setup changes.
## Structures that help
- **Single-sig + separately stored passphrase:** heirs need both pieces; store them with different trusted parties.
- **Multisig (e.g. 2-of-3):** you hold two keys; a trusted person or lawyer holds the third. On your death, the third key plus one of yours allows recovery. Make sure the descriptor is backed up.
- **Collaborative custody services:** some companies hold one key in a multisig and offer inheritance support; you retain control. Evaluate their terms carefully.
## Legal aspects
Mention digital assets in your will. In both the UK and Germany, crypto forms part of the estate. A lawyer familiar with digital assets can help. Never put seed words directly in a will — wills can become public.
## Test it
Ask: could a non-technical relative follow your instructions? If not, simplify.', 'review', 'editorial-team', '{}', '{"faqs": [], "picks": [], "noindex": false, "sources": [], "verdict": ""}', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-13 21:58:13.99779+00', '2026-10-13 22:19:58.877817+00');
INSERT INTO public.settings VALUES ('scoring_weights', '{"value": 5, "backup": 20, "privacy": 15, "features": 10, "security": 35, "usability": 15}', '2026-10-13 21:58:14.000777+00');
